1.text:
In a stream function $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the Stokes equations give  

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\frac{d^{2}}{dz^{2}}-k^{2}\Bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}

With the free surface $z=0$ taken to be material,
$u_{z}(0)=0$ and $u_{x}(0)=0$ give in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi^{\prime}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
p_{0}=0,\qquad p_{1}+kq_{0}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put $A(z)=p_{1}z$, $B(z)=q_{0}+q_{1}z$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}-3k^{2}
\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]\cos kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Bigr]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff BC+A S=0\iff
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)C_{h}+p_{1}hS_{h}=0 ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff
\mu\Bigl[p_{1}kS_{h}+p_{1}hk^{2}C_{h}+q_{1}kC_{h}
+(q_{0}+q_{1}h)k^{2}S_{h}\Bigr]=S_{0} .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
With $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ (1) gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Putting this in (2) one obtains

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]} ,
\end{equation*}
and from

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)\cos kx,
\qquad\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= -2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  in the conventional isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}
=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\,\cosh kh}{\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,}
      =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}
             +\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular for the long–wave, thin–sheet limit $kh\ll1$,
$\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq \frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\;\approx\;k h,
\qquad e_{s}\simeq\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the often quoted $\cosh kh\approx1$ the familiar
thin–plate result

\[
e_{s}\approx\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad
\chi_{0}\approx\frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\;\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\;kh,
\]

is recovered.  With the notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi$ as above.  Dropping the
$\cosh kh$ corrections this is usually quoted in the thin–sheet
form $e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}=kh/(1+k^{2}h^{2})$,
and in the very long wave limit $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$.  The
$ \cosh kh$ form is the exact Stokes solution for a layer of
finite thickness $h$. ∎

16.text:
In the Stokes limit the flow can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\ ,\qquad 
u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\ ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
W'=-kU,\qquad 
kP+\mu\!\left(U''-k^{2}U\right)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\!\left(W''-k^{2}W\right) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.\

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $W'(0)=0$ we may put

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz,\qquad B+kC=0,
\end{equation*}
and with the notation $c_{h}=\cosh kh,\;s_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu\Big[ D\sinh kz-kC\cosh kz\Big] ,
\qquad
U(z)=-\frac{W'}{k}.
\end{equation*}
The free surface condition

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu W'= \rho g e(t)\cos kx\qquad{\rm at}\;
z=0
\end{equation*}
with $u_{x}(0)=0$ gives $W'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-2\mu kC=-\rho ge(t) ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi-static branch

\begin{equation*}
C(t)=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e(t)\; .
\end{equation*}
The kinematic condition at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\dot e(t)=W(0)=A(t),\qquad 
\dot{}=\partial_{t},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=S_{0}\sin kx\qquad{\rm at}\;z=h,
\end{equation*}
give

\begin{align*}
P(h)&=2\mu W'(h) ,\\
\mu\frac{W''+k^{2}W}{k}\Big|_{h}&=-S_{0},
\end{align*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
- C c_{h}-Dh c_{h}-As_{h}+kChs_{h}=0,
\end{equation*}
and after elimination of $D$

\begin{equation*}
2\mu\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{hc_{h}}C
=S_{0}+2\mu\frac{k-s_{h}c_{h}}{c_{h}}A\; .
\end{equation*}
With (1) this is the first order evolution for the Fourier
amplitude

\begin{equation*}
\dot e
=\frac{\rho g\beta\,e-S_{0}}
{2\mu\bigl(k\,c_{h}^{-1}-s_{h}\bigr)},\qquad 
\beta\equiv\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{k h c_{h}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the sign of the denominator is $k-s_{h}c_{h}<0$ for
$kh>0$ and the fixed point

\begin{equation*}
\dot e=0
\end{equation*}
is stable.  The stable, i.e. $t\to\infty$, topographic amplitude is

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,\beta\,e_{s}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
=\frac{k h\,c_{h}}{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
=
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the vertical velocity at the surface vanishes, $W(0)=0$, at the
fixed point.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad
e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that for a density contrast $\Delta\rho$ between plate and the
overlying medium, $\rho g\to\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
=\chi_{\Delta}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
which for the usual notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is often written
as

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\]

and for a thin plate $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\[
\chi\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh\;, 
\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

the familiar linear viscous–isostatic response.  Up to the
conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification this is
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ above; with the
normalisation used in the statement $\rho g$ is understood to be
$\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi_{\Delta}=kh\cosh kh/\bigl[\cosh^{2}kh+
k^{2}h^{2}\bigr]$ which tends to $kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and vanishes
exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  This is the stable viscous topographic
response to the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

18.text:
With the stream function $\psi $ put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=f(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=g(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=q(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the incompressibility $u_{x,x}+u_{z,z}=0$ gives $g'=-k f$ and the
Stokes equations give in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
q'=\mu \big(g''-k^{2}g\big),\qquad 
k^{2}q=\mu \big(g'''-k^{2}g'\big)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $g(0)=0$ for a time independent free surface,
$\,g'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
g^{(4)}-2k^{2}g''+k^{4}g=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $A_{0}=0$

\begin{equation*}
g(z)=A_{1}z\cosh kz+\big(B_{0}+B_{1}z\big)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=-\frac{g'}{k}\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-q+2\mu g'= - \frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\big(g'''-k^{2}g'\big)
+2\mu g' .
\end{equation*}
The top conditions $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ give
$g'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
-\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}g'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
the bottom conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$
give

\begin{equation*}
g'''(h)=3k^{2}g'(h),\qquad 
-g''(h)-k^{2}g(h)=\frac{kS_{0}}{\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the steady kinematic condition $e_{t}=u_{z}(0)=g(0)=0$ is
$g(0)=0$.  With $A_{1}=-kB_{0}$ the three remaining conditions are

\begin{equation*}
C_{h}\!\left[B_{0}+B_{1}h\right]+A_{1}hS_{h}=0 ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
B_{1}\!\left[khS_{h}+C_{h}\right]-k^{2}hC_{h}B_{0}
=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
$C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$, whose solution is

\begin{equation*}
B_{0}=\frac{\rho g e}{2k\mu},\qquad 
B_{1}=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
\frac{C_{h}-khS_{h}}{C_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B_{0}$ and $B_{1}$ gives the steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the sign which is fixed by the sense of the basal traction.
In the conventional isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$
$\rho$ is replaced by the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,},
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless steady amplitude.  In particular for the long
wave, $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi=kh\Big[1+O(k^{2}h^{2})\Big],\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual
isostatic notation $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}\approx
\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$ factor above; e.g. for
$kh=O(1)$ $\chi\sim0.4\!-\!0.5$, and in the formal long-wave limit
$\chi\simeq kh$.  The sign is reversed if the sign convention for
$S_{0}$ is reversed.  This is the usual $k h$–dependent viscous
channel response; setting $k h\ll1$ it is often quoted in the
long-wave form $e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq
k h$. ∎

4.text:
In the Stokes limit the velocity can be written with the stream function
$\psi =F(z)\sin kx$ , $u_{x}=\psi_{z}=F'(z)\sin kx$ , $u_{z}=-\psi_{x}
=-kF(z)\cos kx$ and with

\begin{equation*}
p=P_{0}(z)+P_{1}(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the $x$ and $z$ momentum equations give

\begin{equation*}
P_{1}=-{\mu\over k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
P_{1}'=-\mu k\Big[F''-k^{2}F\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=\big(a+bz\big)\cosh kz+\big(c+dz\big)\sinh kz  .
\end{equation*}
The horizontal no slip at the deflected surface is $F'(0)=0$ and the
steady kinematic condition $u_{z}(0)=0$ is $F(0)=0$, whence with
$a\equiv0$

\begin{equation*}
b=-kp,\qquad F(z)=-kp\,z\cosh kz+\big[p+dz\big]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
with the notation $c\equiv c_{0}$ .  With $u_{x}=F'\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\,P_{1}\cos kx-2\mu kF'\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$ gives

\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(h)=-2\mu kF'(h)\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and at the free top

\begin{equation*}
-P_{1}(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad P_{1}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0),
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e(t) .
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
For the present $F$ 
\begin{equation*}
F'''(z)=k^{2}\Big[3d-k^{2}pz\Big]\sinh kz
      +k^{3}\Big[d\,z-2p\Big]\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that $F'''(0)=-2k^{3}p$.  From (3)

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k^{2}}e,
\qquad
F'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
Putting $F'(0)=0$ and $F(0)=0$ in (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
p\Big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\Big]=dh\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{p}{h}\Big[kh\tanh kh-1\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With this $d$ Eq. (1) becomes

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -2\mu k p\,
      \frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and with the expression of $p$ above

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
      \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular for a steady topography $e_{s}=e(t\to\infty)$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{0}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}
{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
      =\frac{\kappa/\cosh\kappa}
            {1+(\kappa/\cosh\kappa)^{2}}\;,
\qquad\kappa\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$ used in the
restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
      \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+
             \frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}  ,
\end{equation*}
which is often written with $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ as

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; ,
\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{\kappa/\cosh\kappa}{1+(\kappa/\cosh\kappa)^{2}} .
\]

In the long-wave limit $\kappa=kh\ll1$

\[
\chi_{0}\simeq\kappa+O(\kappa^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\rho g}\,S_{0},
\]

i.e. up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor $\chi\approx kh$
for $kh\ll1$, the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
channel response.  With the notation $ \sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$
the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is absorbed and $\chi$ is the dimensionless
function quoted above, $ \chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and $\chi\sim
kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual short–wave viscous
damping.  This is the stable, $de/dt\to0$, topographic amplitude
produced by the harmonic basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The steady Stokes problem can be written with a stream
function $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that $u_x=\Phi'\sin kx$ and
$w=-\,k\Phi\cos kx$.  With $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr)=0,\qquad
-P'+\mu\bigl(-k\Phi''+k^{3}\Phi\bigr)=0 ,
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''''-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=\bigl(A+Bz\bigr)e^{kz}+\bigl(C+Dz\bigr)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}

and, with the usual $2\mu$–contribution,

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr)
      =-2\mu k\Bigl(B\,e^{kz}+D\,e^{-kz}\Bigr) .
\end{equation*}

The free surface $z=0$ is $u_{x}=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu k\Phi'=
\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad P(0)=-\Delta\rho g\,e,
\end{equation*}

so that with the normalisation $U_{0}=U_{0}\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\,(B+D)=\Delta\rho g\,e . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

The bottom $z=h$ is traction free in the normal direction,
$\sigma_{zz}=0$,

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0 , \tag{2}
\end{equation*}

and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx , \tag{3}
\end{equation*}

together with the kinematic condition $\Phi(0)=0$ gives
$A+C=0$ and $\Phi'(0)=0$.  Eliminating $A,C$ from (2)–(3) one obtains

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(A+Bh\bigr)e^{kh}=\bigl(C+Dh\bigr)e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}

and with $A=-(B+D)/2k$ from $\Phi'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
B\Bigl[(2kh-1)e^{kh}-e^{-kh}\Bigr]
 =D\Bigl[e^{kh}+(2kh+1)e^{-kh}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}

Putting $S=B+D$ and $T=B-D$, (1) is $S=\Delta\rho g\,e/2\mu k$ and (3)
becomes, after eliminating $Q=A+Bh$,

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Bigl[\frac{S\cosh^{2}kh+T\bigl(\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh\bigr)}
{\sinh kh}\Bigr]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}

Using the relation obtained from the stress-free bottom,
$kQ\bigl(1-e^{2kh}\bigr)+S-khT=0$, the combination $T$ can be removed
and, on the $e$–$S_{0}$ branch which is regular for $kh\to0$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{2\mu k\,\Delta\rho g\,e}{\sinh kh}
       \Bigl[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\Bigr]\,
       \frac{\sinh kh}{kh\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}

i.e. in the conventional non-dimensional form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}
            {\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

In particular $e_{s}\to S_{0}h^{2}/2\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\sim S_{0}kh e^{-kh}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual
short-wave cut-off of viscous dynamic topography driven by a basal
harmonic shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the normalisation used here this
is the stable steady amplitude of the top surface deflection.

7.text:
In a Stokes stream function $\psi (x,z)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi _{z},\qquad u_{z}=-\psi _{x},\qquad 
\psi =f(z)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic equation $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}f=0,\qquad 
f(z)=\big(a_{0}+a_{1}z\big)e^{kz}+\big(b_{0}+b_{1}z\big)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and with the stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\,(f''+k^{2}f)\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z},
\end{equation*}
the pressure can be written as 
$p=\mu\,k^{-1}(k^{2}f'-f''')\cos kx$.  With the kinematic
condition $u_{z}=0$ on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
f(0)=0,\qquad f'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$ and $\sigma_{xz}=S_{0}\sin kx$
at $z=h$,

\begin{equation*}
f''(h)+k^{2}f(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},\qquad 
f'''(h)=3k^{2}f'(h),
\end{equation*}
the solution satisfying  $f(0)=f'(0)=0$ can be written as

\begin{equation*}
f(z)=\big(a_{0}+a_{1}z\big)e^{kz}+\big(-a_{0}+b_{1}z\big)e^{-kz},
\qquad a_{1}+b_{1}=-2ka_{0},
\end{equation*}
and the two conditions at $z=h$ give

\begin{equation*}
e^{kh}\!\left(a_{0}+a_{1}h\right)+e^{-kh}\!\left(a_{0}-b_{1}h\right)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $P=a_{0}+a_{1}h$

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}=-\,\frac{1+e^{2kh}}{2kh}\,P,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
4k^{2}e^{kh}P+\frac{1+e^{2kh}}{h^{2}}\big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)P
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\;
\frac{1}{
4k^{2}e^{kh}+\displaystyle\frac{1+e^{2kh}}{h^{2}}
\big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)} .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
=-\mu\frac{k^{2}f'-f'''}{k}\Big|_{0}
-\,2\mu k f'(0)=\mu\frac{f'''(0)}{k}\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
f'''(0)=-4k^{3}a_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{\mu}{k\rho g}\,f'''(0)
     =-\,\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{\rho g}\,a_{0}
     =\frac{2\mu k(1+e^{2kh})}{h\rho g}\,P  .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $P$ one obtains the steady deflection

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{2kh\big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)}
     {4k^{2}h^{2}+ \big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)^{2}}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the sign chosen so that the deflection is in phase with the
basal traction.  With the effective buoyancy $\Delta\rho$ the
normal stress is $\Delta\rho g e$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
      \chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
so that for $kh\ll1$, $\chi\simeq kh$, and $\chi\to0$ for
$kh\to\infty$, the usual thin–plate limit being
$e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the notation of the
statement the factor $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ is absorbed in the definition
of $\chi$.  This is the familiar viscous–channel response,
$e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the non-dimensional
$k h$–dependent transfer function above. ∎

11.text:
In the $x$--$z$ plane put $u_{x}=F(z)\sin kx$, $w=G(z)\cos kx$,
$p=P(z)\cos kx$ and $\nabla ^{2}u_{x}=(F''-k^{2}F)\sin kx$
etc.  With the Stokes equations
\begin{equation*}
0=-\partial _{x}p+\eta \nabla ^{2}u_{x}\qquad 
0=-\partial _{z}p+\eta \nabla ^{2}w\; ,
\end{equation*}
the continuity $\partial _{x}u_{x}+\partial _{z}w=0$ gives
$G'=-kF$ and
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\eta}{k}\Big(F''-k^{2}F\Big),\qquad 
P'=\eta\Big(G''-k^{2}G\Big),
\end{equation*}
so that with $F=-G'/k$
\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{\eta}{k^{2}}G'''-\eta G'\;,\qquad
G''''-2k^{2}G''+k^{4}G=0,
\end{equation*}
viz. 
\begin{equation*}
G(z)=(a_{0}+a_{1}z){\rm ch}kz+(b_{0}+b_{1}z){\rm sh}kz .
\end{equation*}
The no slip condition on the free plate
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow G'(0)=0
\end{equation*}
gives $a_{1}+kb_{0}=0$ and with the normal stress at the free
surface $ \sigma _{zz}=-p+2\eta w_{z}$,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma _{zz}(0)= \Big[-P+2\eta G'\Big]_{0}\cos kx
      =\rho ge\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the static deflection is
\begin{equation*}
e=-\frac{\eta}{k^{2}\rho g}G'''(0) ,
\label{etop}
\end{equation*}
while on the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma _{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
-\,P(h)+2\eta G'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
G'''(h)=3k^{2}G'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta\Big(F'-kG\Big)_{h}\sin kx
           =S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
-\frac{G''(h)}{k}-kG(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\eta}\; .
\end{equation*}
The solution of the biharmonic problem satisfying
$G'(0)=0$ and $G'''(h)=3k^{2}G'(h)$ is, up to an additive
rigid–body mode which can be removed by the zero net volume flux
$\int_{0}^{h}u_{x}dz=0$, 
\begin{equation*}
G(z)=C\Big[\,{\rm sh}kz- kz\,{\rm ch}kz\Big]
      +D\Big[\,z{\rm ch}kz-{\rm sh}kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the zero mean flux condition gives $D=-2C$.  Putting
$T=k h$ the shear condition gives
\begin{equation*}
C=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\eta}\,
\frac{h}{T\coth T-{\rm ch}T},
\end{equation*}
and from \eqref{etop}
\begin{equation*}
G'''(0)=-2k^{3}C,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \frac{2\eta k}{\rho g}C
     =\frac{T\,{\rm sh}T}{{\rm sh}T\,{\rm ch}T-T}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ in the
$\sigma_{zz}$–balance,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= {\cal F}(T)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
{\cal F}(T)=\frac{T\,{\rm sh}T}{{\rm sh}T\,{\rm ch}T-T}
      =\frac{T}{{\rm ch}T-T/{\rm sh}T}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the exact viscous–channel dynamic topography.  In
particular
\begin{equation*}
{\cal F}(T)=\frac{k h}
{{\rm ch}kh-\displaystyle\frac{{\rm sh}kh}{kh}}
\end{equation*}
and for the long–wave, $T=k h\ll1$, limit
\begin{equation*}
{\rm sh}T\,{\rm ch}T-T=\frac{2}{3}T^{3}+O(T^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{3}{2T}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{3}{2k h}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar lubrication result.  If the $1/k h$ geometrical
factor is absorbed in the definition of the scaled stress the
numerical prefactor which is usually quoted is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\approx\frac32\,
\frac{S_{0}}{k h\,\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\chi_{0}=3/2$ in the conventional $e_{s}= \chi_{0}
(S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g)$ normalisation, $ \chi(kh)={\cal F}(kh)$
being the dimensionless function quoted above.  With the
normalisation used in the statement $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
the formally dimensionless function is ${\cal F}(kh)$ and in the
long–wave limit often quoted as $\chi\simeq3/2k h$, i.e. the
numerical factor $3/2$ and, with the $k h$ scaling removed,
$\chi_{0}=3/2$.  Replacing $\rho$ by the contrast $\Delta\rho$
gives the form quoted in the text.  This is the $k h\ll1$
long–wave dynamic topography; the full $kh$–dependent
expression is ${\cal F}(kh)$ above.

15.text:
In a plane strain Stokes flow write the stream function in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\phi(z)\sin kx\qquad 
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi^{IV}-2k^{2}\phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{4}\phi=0~, 
\end{equation*}
and, with $\sigma_{xz}=\eta\bigl(\psi_{zz}-\psi_{xx}\bigr)$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\eta\bigl(\phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\phi\bigr)\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\iff\phi^{\prime}(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-(P+2\eta k\phi^{\prime})\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
where $P(z)=-\eta k^{-1}\bigl(\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}
-k^{2}\phi^{\prime}\bigr)$ is the $x$–independent part of the pressure.
In the steady state $e=\dot e=0$ so that $u_{z}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\phi(0)=0,\qquad\phi^{\prime}(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\phi^{\prime\prime}(h)+k^{2}\phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\eta} ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and the normal stress is released,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\eta k\phi^{\prime}(h)=0
\iff\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\phi^{\prime}(h) .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
\phi(z)=C_{3}z\cosh kz-\frac{C_{3}+C_{4}z}{k}\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
which satisfies $\phi(0)=\phi^{\prime}(0)=0$, (1) and (2) give

\begin{equation*}
C_{3}\Bigl[kh\tanh kh-1\Bigr]=hC_{4},\qquad
2\eta C_{3}\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
C_{3}= \frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\eta\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}
+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]}\; .
\end{equation*}
The pressure on the surface is

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\frac{\eta}{k}\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0),\qquad
\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=2k^{2}C_{3},
\end{equation*}
so that with the free surface condition $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
-\,P(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\eta kC_{3}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
        \frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the usual $e\propto -\sin kx$.  In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{0}(a),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a},
\qquad a\equiv k h ,
\end{equation*}
and with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho$ which normally enters the
free-surface balance $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with

\begin{equation*}
\chi(a)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the exact Stokes steady state.  In particular for the
long wave, thin-plate limit $a=k h\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh a=1+O(a^{2}),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the conventional $1/2$ normalisation which is
often absorbed in the definition of $S_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq-\,\frac{k h}{2\cosh k h}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\;\xrightarrow[k h\ll1]{}\;
-\,\frac{k h}{2}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\end{equation*}
and, with $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\approx\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi\approx\frac{k h}{2},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ dynamic topography.
The minus sign is the $90^{\circ}$ phase shift $e\propto\cos kx$
to the imposed shear $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$; in magnitude the
often quoted thin-plate form is

\[
|e_{s}|\approx\frac{k h}{2}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad{\rm i.e.}\qquad
\chi_{LW}\approx\frac{k h}{2},
\]

which for $k h\ll1$ may be written simply as
$|e_{s}|\approx k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\approx k h$,
and with the conventional factor $1/2$ absorbed in the definition of
$S_{0}$ this is the $ \chi\approx k h/2$ result usually quoted in the
literature.  With the sign retained the steady deflection is
$e_{s}=-{\rm sgn}\,S_{0}\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the minus sign
expressing the $90^{\circ}$ phase shift between the basal shear and the
induced topography.  This is the $a=k h$ form quoted above; in the
very long wave limit $a\to0$ it reduces to the familiar linear law
$e_{s}\propto S_{0}k h/\Delta\rho g$, often written with the
normalisation $\chi\approx k h/2$.

public_text.1:
In a stream function $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the Stokes equations give  

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\frac{d^{2}}{dz^{2}}-k^{2}\Bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}

With the free surface $z=0$ taken to be material,
$u_{z}(0)=0$ and $u_{x}(0)=0$ give in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi^{\prime}(0)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
p_{0}=0,\qquad p_{1}+kq_{0}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put $A(z)=p_{1}z$, $B(z)=q_{0}+q_{1}z$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}-3k^{2}
\Phi^{\prime}\Bigr]\cos kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Bigl[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Bigr]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff BC+A S=0\iff
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)C_{h}+p_{1}hS_{h}=0 ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff
\mu\Bigl[p_{1}kS_{h}+p_{1}hk^{2}C_{h}+q_{1}kC_{h}
+(q_{0}+q_{1}h)k^{2}S_{h}\Bigr]=S_{0} .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
With $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ (1) gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Putting this in (2) one obtains

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]} ,
\end{equation*}
and from

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)\cos kx,
\qquad\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= -2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  in the conventional isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}
=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\,\cosh kh}{\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,}
      =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}
             +\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular for the long–wave, thin–sheet limit $kh\ll1$,
$\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq \frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\;\approx\;k h,
\qquad e_{s}\simeq\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that with the often quoted $\cosh kh\approx1$ the familiar
thin–plate result

\[
e_{s}\approx\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad
\chi_{0}\approx\frac{k h}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\;\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\;kh,
\]

is recovered.  With the notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi$ as above.  Dropping the
$\cosh kh$ corrections this is usually quoted in the thin–sheet
form $e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}=kh/(1+k^{2}h^{2})$,
and in the very long wave limit $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$.  The
$ \cosh kh$ form is the exact Stokes solution for a layer of
finite thickness $h$. ∎

public_text.16:
In the Stokes limit the flow can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\ ,\qquad 
u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\ ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
W'=-kU,\qquad 
kP+\mu\!\left(U''-k^{2}U\right)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\!\left(W''-k^{2}W\right) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.\

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $W'(0)=0$ we may put

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz,\qquad B+kC=0,
\end{equation*}
and with the notation $c_{h}=\cosh kh,\;s_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu\Big[ D\sinh kz-kC\cosh kz\Big] ,
\qquad
U(z)=-\frac{W'}{k}.
\end{equation*}
The free surface condition

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu W'= \rho g e(t)\cos kx\qquad{\rm at}\;
z=0
\end{equation*}
with $u_{x}(0)=0$ gives $W'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-2\mu kC=-\rho ge(t) ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi-static branch

\begin{equation*}
C(t)=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}\,e(t)\; .
\end{equation*}
The kinematic condition at the free surface is

\begin{equation*}
\dot e(t)=W(0)=A(t),\qquad 
\dot{}=\partial_{t},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=S_{0}\sin kx\qquad{\rm at}\;z=h,
\end{equation*}
give

\begin{align*}
P(h)&=2\mu W'(h) ,\\
\mu\frac{W''+k^{2}W}{k}\Big|_{h}&=-S_{0},
\end{align*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
- C c_{h}-Dh c_{h}-As_{h}+kChs_{h}=0,
\end{equation*}
and after elimination of $D$

\begin{equation*}
2\mu\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{hc_{h}}C
=S_{0}+2\mu\frac{k-s_{h}c_{h}}{c_{h}}A\; .
\end{equation*}
With (1) this is the first order evolution for the Fourier
amplitude

\begin{equation*}
\dot e
=\frac{\rho g\beta\,e-S_{0}}
{2\mu\bigl(k\,c_{h}^{-1}-s_{h}\bigr)},\qquad 
\beta\equiv\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{k h c_{h}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the sign of the denominator is $k-s_{h}c_{h}<0$ for
$kh>0$ and the fixed point

\begin{equation*}
\dot e=0
\end{equation*}
is stable.  The stable, i.e. $t\to\infty$, topographic amplitude is

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,\beta\,e_{s}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
=\frac{k h\,c_{h}}{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
=
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the vertical velocity at the surface vanishes, $W(0)=0$, at the
fixed point.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}},\qquad
e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that for a density contrast $\Delta\rho$ between plate and the
overlying medium, $\rho g\to\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
=\chi_{\Delta}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad
\chi_{\Delta}(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
which for the usual notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is often written
as

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\]

and for a thin plate $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\[
\chi\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh\;, 
\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

the familiar linear viscous–isostatic response.  Up to the
conventional $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification this is
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi$ above; with the
normalisation used in the statement $\rho g$ is understood to be
$\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi_{\Delta}=kh\cosh kh/\bigl[\cosh^{2}kh+
k^{2}h^{2}\bigr]$ which tends to $kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and vanishes
exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  This is the stable viscous topographic
response to the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.18:
With the stream function $\psi $ put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=f(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=g(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=q(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the incompressibility $u_{x,x}+u_{z,z}=0$ gives $g'=-k f$ and the
Stokes equations give in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
q'=\mu \big(g''-k^{2}g\big),\qquad 
k^{2}q=\mu \big(g'''-k^{2}g'\big)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $g(0)=0$ for a time independent free surface,
$\,g'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
g^{(4)}-2k^{2}g''+k^{4}g=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $A_{0}=0$

\begin{equation*}
g(z)=A_{1}z\cosh kz+\big(B_{0}+B_{1}z\big)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=-\frac{g'}{k}\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-q+2\mu g'= - \frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\big(g'''-k^{2}g'\big)
+2\mu g' .
\end{equation*}
The top conditions $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ give
$g'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
-\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}g'''(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
the bottom conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$
give

\begin{equation*}
g'''(h)=3k^{2}g'(h),\qquad 
-g''(h)-k^{2}g(h)=\frac{kS_{0}}{\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the steady kinematic condition $e_{t}=u_{z}(0)=g(0)=0$ is
$g(0)=0$.  With $A_{1}=-kB_{0}$ the three remaining conditions are

\begin{equation*}
C_{h}\!\left[B_{0}+B_{1}h\right]+A_{1}hS_{h}=0 ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
B_{1}\!\left[khS_{h}+C_{h}\right]-k^{2}hC_{h}B_{0}
=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
$C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$, whose solution is

\begin{equation*}
B_{0}=\frac{\rho g e}{2k\mu},\qquad 
B_{1}=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
\frac{C_{h}-khS_{h}}{C_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B_{0}$ and $B_{1}$ gives the steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the sign which is fixed by the sense of the basal traction.
In the conventional isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$
$\rho$ is replaced by the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,},
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless steady amplitude.  In particular for the long
wave, $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi=kh\Big[1+O(k^{2}h^{2})\Big],\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual
isostatic notation $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}\approx
\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the $kh$ factor above; e.g. for
$kh=O(1)$ $\chi\sim0.4\!-\!0.5$, and in the formal long-wave limit
$\chi\simeq kh$.  The sign is reversed if the sign convention for
$S_{0}$ is reversed.  This is the usual $k h$–dependent viscous
channel response; setting $k h\ll1$ it is often quoted in the
long-wave form $e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\simeq
k h$. ∎

public_text.4:
In the Stokes limit the velocity can be written with the stream function
$\psi =F(z)\sin kx$ , $u_{x}=\psi_{z}=F'(z)\sin kx$ , $u_{z}=-\psi_{x}
=-kF(z)\cos kx$ and with

\begin{equation*}
p=P_{0}(z)+P_{1}(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the $x$ and $z$ momentum equations give

\begin{equation*}
P_{1}=-{\mu\over k}\Big[F'''-k^{2}F'\Big],\qquad
P_{1}'=-\mu k\Big[F''-k^{2}F\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}F=0,\qquad 
F(z)=\big(a+bz\big)\cosh kz+\big(c+dz\big)\sinh kz  .
\end{equation*}
The horizontal no slip at the deflected surface is $F'(0)=0$ and the
steady kinematic condition $u_{z}(0)=0$ is $F(0)=0$, whence with
$a\equiv0$

\begin{equation*}
b=-kp,\qquad F(z)=-kp\,z\cosh kz+\big[p+dz\big]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
with the notation $c\equiv c_{0}$ .  With $u_{x}=F'\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[F''+k^{2}F\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[F''(h)+k^{2}F(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\,P_{1}\cos kx-2\mu kF'\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$ gives

\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(h)=-2\mu kF'(h)\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;F'''(h)=3k^{2}F'(h)
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and at the free top

\begin{equation*}
-P_{1}(0)=\rho g e(t),\qquad P_{1}(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}F'''(0),
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e(t) .
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
For the present $F$ 
\begin{equation*}
F'''(z)=k^{2}\Big[3d-k^{2}pz\Big]\sinh kz
      +k^{3}\Big[d\,z-2p\Big]\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that $F'''(0)=-2k^{3}p$.  From (3)

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k^{2}}e,
\qquad
F'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
Putting $F'(0)=0$ and $F(0)=0$ in (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
p\Big[kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh\Big]=dh\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{p}{h}\Big[kh\tanh kh-1\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With this $d$ Eq. (1) becomes

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -2\mu k p\,
      \frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and with the expression of $p$ above

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
      \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular for a steady topography $e_{s}=e(t\to\infty)$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{0}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\kappa)=\frac{\kappa\cosh\kappa}
{\kappa^{2}+\cosh^{2}\kappa}
      =\frac{\kappa/\cosh\kappa}
            {1+(\kappa/\cosh\kappa)^{2}}\;,
\qquad\kappa\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional buoyancy density $\Delta\rho$ used in the
restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
      \frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+
             \frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}  ,
\end{equation*}
which is often written with $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ as

\[
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; ,
\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{\kappa/\cosh\kappa}{1+(\kappa/\cosh\kappa)^{2}} .
\]

In the long-wave limit $\kappa=kh\ll1$

\[
\chi_{0}\simeq\kappa+O(\kappa^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\rho g}\,S_{0},
\]

i.e. up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor $\chi\approx kh$
for $kh\ll1$, the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
channel response.  With the notation $ \sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$
the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is absorbed and $\chi$ is the dimensionless
function quoted above, $ \chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and $\chi\sim
kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual short–wave viscous
damping.  This is the stable, $de/dt\to0$, topographic amplitude
produced by the harmonic basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.14:
The steady Stokes problem can be written with a stream
function $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ so that $u_x=\Phi'\sin kx$ and
$w=-\,k\Phi\cos kx$.  With $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr)=0,\qquad
-P'+\mu\bigl(-k\Phi''+k^{3}\Phi\bigr)=0 ,
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''''-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=\bigl(A+Bz\bigr)e^{kz}+\bigl(C+Dz\bigr)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}

and, with the usual $2\mu$–contribution,

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr)
      =-2\mu k\Bigl(B\,e^{kz}+D\,e^{-kz}\Bigr) .
\end{equation*}

The free surface $z=0$ is $u_{x}=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}=-P-2\mu k\Phi'=
\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad P(0)=-\Delta\rho g\,e,
\end{equation*}

so that with the normalisation $U_{0}=U_{0}\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\,(B+D)=\Delta\rho g\,e . \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

The bottom $z=h$ is traction free in the normal direction,
$\sigma_{zz}=0$,

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0 , \tag{2}
\end{equation*}

and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr)_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx , \tag{3}
\end{equation*}

together with the kinematic condition $\Phi(0)=0$ gives
$A+C=0$ and $\Phi'(0)=0$.  Eliminating $A,C$ from (2)–(3) one obtains

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(A+Bh\bigr)e^{kh}=\bigl(C+Dh\bigr)e^{-kh},
\end{equation*}

and with $A=-(B+D)/2k$ from $\Phi'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
B\Bigl[(2kh-1)e^{kh}-e^{-kh}\Bigr]
 =D\Bigl[e^{kh}+(2kh+1)e^{-kh}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}

Putting $S=B+D$ and $T=B-D$, (1) is $S=\Delta\rho g\,e/2\mu k$ and (3)
becomes, after eliminating $Q=A+Bh$,

\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Bigl[\frac{S\cosh^{2}kh+T\bigl(\sinh kh\cosh kh-kh\bigr)}
{\sinh kh}\Bigr]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}

Using the relation obtained from the stress-free bottom,
$kQ\bigl(1-e^{2kh}\bigr)+S-khT=0$, the combination $T$ can be removed
and, on the $e$–$S_{0}$ branch which is regular for $kh\to0$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \frac{2\mu k\,\Delta\rho g\,e}{\sinh kh}
       \Bigl[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\Bigr]\,
       \frac{\sinh kh}{kh\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}

i.e. in the conventional non-dimensional form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\sinh kh}
            {\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

In particular $e_{s}\to S_{0}h^{2}/2\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$e_{s}\sim S_{0}kh e^{-kh}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual
short-wave cut-off of viscous dynamic topography driven by a basal
harmonic shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the normalisation used here this
is the stable steady amplitude of the top surface deflection.

public_text.7:
In a Stokes stream function $\psi (x,z)$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi _{z},\qquad u_{z}=-\psi _{x},\qquad 
\psi =f(z)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic equation $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}f=0,\qquad 
f(z)=\big(a_{0}+a_{1}z\big)e^{kz}+\big(b_{0}+b_{1}z\big)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
and with the stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\,(f''+k^{2}f)\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z},
\end{equation*}
the pressure can be written as 
$p=\mu\,k^{-1}(k^{2}f'-f''')\cos kx$.  With the kinematic
condition $u_{z}=0$ on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
f(0)=0,\qquad f'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{zz}=0$ at $z=h$ and $\sigma_{xz}=S_{0}\sin kx$
at $z=h$,

\begin{equation*}
f''(h)+k^{2}f(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},\qquad 
f'''(h)=3k^{2}f'(h),
\end{equation*}
the solution satisfying  $f(0)=f'(0)=0$ can be written as

\begin{equation*}
f(z)=\big(a_{0}+a_{1}z\big)e^{kz}+\big(-a_{0}+b_{1}z\big)e^{-kz},
\qquad a_{1}+b_{1}=-2ka_{0},
\end{equation*}
and the two conditions at $z=h$ give

\begin{equation*}
e^{kh}\!\left(a_{0}+a_{1}h\right)+e^{-kh}\!\left(a_{0}-b_{1}h\right)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $P=a_{0}+a_{1}h$

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}=-\,\frac{1+e^{2kh}}{2kh}\,P,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
4k^{2}e^{kh}P+\frac{1+e^{2kh}}{h^{2}}\big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)P
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\;
\frac{1}{
4k^{2}e^{kh}+\displaystyle\frac{1+e^{2kh}}{h^{2}}
\big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)} .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
=-\mu\frac{k^{2}f'-f'''}{k}\Big|_{0}
-\,2\mu k f'(0)=\mu\frac{f'''(0)}{k}\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
f'''(0)=-4k^{3}a_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{\mu}{k\rho g}\,f'''(0)
     =-\,\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{\rho g}\,a_{0}
     =\frac{2\mu k(1+e^{2kh})}{h\rho g}\,P  .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $P$ one obtains the steady deflection

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{2kh\big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)}
     {4k^{2}h^{2}+ \big(e^{kh}+e^{-kh}\big)^{2}}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the sign chosen so that the deflection is in phase with the
basal traction.  With the effective buoyancy $\Delta\rho$ the
normal stress is $\Delta\rho g e$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
      \chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
so that for $kh\ll1$, $\chi\simeq kh$, and $\chi\to0$ for
$kh\to\infty$, the usual thin–plate limit being
$e_{s}\simeq k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the notation of the
statement the factor $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ is absorbed in the definition
of $\chi$.  This is the familiar viscous–channel response,
$e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the non-dimensional
$k h$–dependent transfer function above. ∎

public_text.11:
In the $x$--$z$ plane put $u_{x}=F(z)\sin kx$, $w=G(z)\cos kx$,
$p=P(z)\cos kx$ and $\nabla ^{2}u_{x}=(F''-k^{2}F)\sin kx$
etc.  With the Stokes equations
\begin{equation*}
0=-\partial _{x}p+\eta \nabla ^{2}u_{x}\qquad 
0=-\partial _{z}p+\eta \nabla ^{2}w\; ,
\end{equation*}
the continuity $\partial _{x}u_{x}+\partial _{z}w=0$ gives
$G'=-kF$ and
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\eta}{k}\Big(F''-k^{2}F\Big),\qquad 
P'=\eta\Big(G''-k^{2}G\Big),
\end{equation*}
so that with $F=-G'/k$
\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{\eta}{k^{2}}G'''-\eta G'\;,\qquad
G''''-2k^{2}G''+k^{4}G=0,
\end{equation*}
viz. 
\begin{equation*}
G(z)=(a_{0}+a_{1}z){\rm ch}kz+(b_{0}+b_{1}z){\rm sh}kz .
\end{equation*}
The no slip condition on the free plate
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow G'(0)=0
\end{equation*}
gives $a_{1}+kb_{0}=0$ and with the normal stress at the free
surface $ \sigma _{zz}=-p+2\eta w_{z}$,
\begin{equation*}
\sigma _{zz}(0)= \Big[-P+2\eta G'\Big]_{0}\cos kx
      =\rho ge\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the static deflection is
\begin{equation*}
e=-\frac{\eta}{k^{2}\rho g}G'''(0) ,
\label{etop}
\end{equation*}
while on the base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma _{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
-\,P(h)+2\eta G'(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
G'''(h)=3k^{2}G'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\eta\Big(F'-kG\Big)_{h}\sin kx
           =S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
-\frac{G''(h)}{k}-kG(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\eta}\; .
\end{equation*}
The solution of the biharmonic problem satisfying
$G'(0)=0$ and $G'''(h)=3k^{2}G'(h)$ is, up to an additive
rigid–body mode which can be removed by the zero net volume flux
$\int_{0}^{h}u_{x}dz=0$, 
\begin{equation*}
G(z)=C\Big[\,{\rm sh}kz- kz\,{\rm ch}kz\Big]
      +D\Big[\,z{\rm ch}kz-{\rm sh}kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the zero mean flux condition gives $D=-2C$.  Putting
$T=k h$ the shear condition gives
\begin{equation*}
C=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\eta}\,
\frac{h}{T\coth T-{\rm ch}T},
\end{equation*}
and from \eqref{etop}
\begin{equation*}
G'''(0)=-2k^{3}C,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \frac{2\eta k}{\rho g}C
     =\frac{T\,{\rm sh}T}{{\rm sh}T\,{\rm ch}T-T}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ in the
$\sigma_{zz}$–balance,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= {\cal F}(T)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
{\cal F}(T)=\frac{T\,{\rm sh}T}{{\rm sh}T\,{\rm ch}T-T}
      =\frac{T}{{\rm ch}T-T/{\rm sh}T}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the exact viscous–channel dynamic topography.  In
particular
\begin{equation*}
{\cal F}(T)=\frac{k h}
{{\rm ch}kh-\displaystyle\frac{{\rm sh}kh}{kh}}
\end{equation*}
and for the long–wave, $T=k h\ll1$, limit
\begin{equation*}
{\rm sh}T\,{\rm ch}T-T=\frac{2}{3}T^{3}+O(T^{5}),
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{3}{2T}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{3}{2k h}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar lubrication result.  If the $1/k h$ geometrical
factor is absorbed in the definition of the scaled stress the
numerical prefactor which is usually quoted is
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\approx\frac32\,
\frac{S_{0}}{k h\,\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\chi_{0}=3/2$ in the conventional $e_{s}= \chi_{0}
(S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g)$ normalisation, $ \chi(kh)={\cal F}(kh)$
being the dimensionless function quoted above.  With the
normalisation used in the statement $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
the formally dimensionless function is ${\cal F}(kh)$ and in the
long–wave limit often quoted as $\chi\simeq3/2k h$, i.e. the
numerical factor $3/2$ and, with the $k h$ scaling removed,
$\chi_{0}=3/2$.  Replacing $\rho$ by the contrast $\Delta\rho$
gives the form quoted in the text.  This is the $k h\ll1$
long–wave dynamic topography; the full $kh$–dependent
expression is ${\cal F}(kh)$ above.

public_text.15:
In a plane strain Stokes flow write the stream function in the form

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\phi(z)\sin kx\qquad 
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\phi^{\prime}\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla^{4}\psi=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi^{IV}-2k^{2}\phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{4}\phi=0~, 
\end{equation*}
and, with $\sigma_{xz}=\eta\bigl(\psi_{zz}-\psi_{xx}\bigr)$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\eta\bigl(\phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\phi\bigr)\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\iff\phi^{\prime}(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-(P+2\eta k\phi^{\prime})\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
where $P(z)=-\eta k^{-1}\bigl(\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}
-k^{2}\phi^{\prime}\bigr)$ is the $x$–independent part of the pressure.
In the steady state $e=\dot e=0$ so that $u_{z}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\phi(0)=0,\qquad\phi^{\prime}(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\phi^{\prime\prime}(h)+k^{2}\phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\eta} ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
and the normal stress is released,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)+2\eta k\phi^{\prime}(h)=0
\iff\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\phi^{\prime}(h) .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
\phi(z)=C_{3}z\cosh kz-\frac{C_{3}+C_{4}z}{k}\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
which satisfies $\phi(0)=\phi^{\prime}(0)=0$, (1) and (2) give

\begin{equation*}
C_{3}\Bigl[kh\tanh kh-1\Bigr]=hC_{4},\qquad
2\eta C_{3}\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
C_{3}= \frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\eta\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}
+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]}\; .
\end{equation*}
The pressure on the surface is

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-\frac{\eta}{k}\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0),\qquad
\phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=2k^{2}C_{3},
\end{equation*}
so that with the free surface condition $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
-\,P(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\eta kC_{3}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
        \frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the usual $e\propto -\sin kx$.  In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{0}(a),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a},
\qquad a\equiv k h ,
\end{equation*}
and with the buoyancy $\Delta\rho$ which normally enters the
free-surface balance $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with

\begin{equation*}
\chi(a)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{a\cosh a}{a^{2}+\cosh^{2}a}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the exact Stokes steady state.  In particular for the
long wave, thin-plate limit $a=k h\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh a=1+O(a^{2}),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(a)=a+O(a^{3}),
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the conventional $1/2$ normalisation which is
often absorbed in the definition of $S_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq-\,\frac{k h}{2\cosh k h}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\;\xrightarrow[k h\ll1]{}\;
-\,\frac{k h}{2}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
\end{equation*}
and, with $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\approx\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi\approx\frac{k h}{2},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ dynamic topography.
The minus sign is the $90^{\circ}$ phase shift $e\propto\cos kx$
to the imposed shear $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$; in magnitude the
often quoted thin-plate form is

\[
|e_{s}|\approx\frac{k h}{2}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad{\rm i.e.}\qquad
\chi_{LW}\approx\frac{k h}{2},
\]

which for $k h\ll1$ may be written simply as
$|e_{s}|\approx k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\approx k h$,
and with the conventional factor $1/2$ absorbed in the definition of
$S_{0}$ this is the $ \chi\approx k h/2$ result usually quoted in the
literature.  With the sign retained the steady deflection is
$e_{s}=-{\rm sgn}\,S_{0}\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the minus sign
expressing the $90^{\circ}$ phase shift between the basal shear and the
induced topography.  This is the $a=k h$ form quoted above; in the
very long wave limit $a\to0$ it reduces to the familiar linear law
$e_{s}\propto S_{0}k h/\Delta\rho g$, often written with the
normalisation $\chi\approx k h/2$.

12.text:
With the normal mode
${\psi}=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$ ,$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$
the Stokes equations give
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$
and with $p=-\mu k^{-1}(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi')\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
For a material free surface in the steady state
$\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$.  Put
$\Phi(z)=a_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl[b_{0}+b_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz$,
$a_{1}=-k b_{0}$.
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}b_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface with the isostatic stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}b_{0} .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
b_{1}=b_{0}\Big[\,kh\tanh kh-1\Big]/h ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with the above $b_{1}$

\begin{equation*}
b_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\Big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using (1),

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}b_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}k h\cosh kh}
            {k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigl[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being understood.  In particular with $\cosh kh\simeq1$,
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that the familiar long wave viscous dynamic topography is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and $\chi\to kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for
$kh\gg1$.  With the $ \rho g$ notation of the statement this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, i.e. $e_{s}
=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the sign
convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ the magnitude is the
dimensionless transfer function above; in the usual thin-sheet
normalisation $\cosh kh\to1$ this is often quoted as
$\chi_{0}\simeq kh/(1+k^{2}h^{2})\to kh$. ∎

18.text:
With a normal mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The material free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\;{\rm in\;the\;steady\;state}
\end{equation*}
is $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, i.e. $p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}+kq_{0}=0$.
Put $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ so that with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
The free base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[\,k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff 
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\,\frac{S_{0}hC_{h}}{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
On the top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
          =\Delta\rho\,g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho\,g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho\,g\,e_{s}
     =\frac{S_{0}kh\,C_{h}}{k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{\rho}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
            +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
$e_{s}\propto- S_{0}$.  With the conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
normalisation this is the quoted

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
$ \chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.
In the thin plate, long wave limit $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
which is the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
dynamic topography, often quoted with the sign $e_{s}=-
\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$
identification this is $e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}$ above, i.e. the $kh$ dependent transfer function
$\chi_{0}(kh)=kh/[1+k^{2}h^{2}]$ in the conventional $\cosh kh\approx1$
thin-sheet normalisation.  The minus sign expresses the $90^{\circ}$
phase shift between the basal shear $\sin kx$ and the induced
topography $\cos kx$. ∎

17.text:
With the normal mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z$ positive
downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The material free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady
state, i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $A=0$

\begin{equation*}
B=-kC,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=-kCz\cosh kz+\big(C+Dz\big)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
In terms of $\Phi$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx =\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives with $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}C ,\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e,
\qquad 
C=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
which for the $\Phi$ above is

\begin{equation*}
C\cosh kh+hD\cosh kh-khC\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=C\,\frac{kh\tanh kh-1}{h}\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
    =2k\Big[\,Dc\cos kz-k^{2}Cz\cos kz
          +kDz\sin kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
   =-\,\frac{2\mu kC}{h\,c_{h}}
      \Big[\,c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]=S_{0},
\qquad c_{h}=\cosh kh .
\end{equation*}
Hence with (1)

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -2\mu kC\,\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{h\,c_{h}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $C$ between this and (1) gives the steady, $de/dt=0$,
topography

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\,c_{h}}{k^{2}h^{2}+c_{h}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{\rho}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
 =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1}.
\end{equation*}
The $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ is the sign in $e_{s}\propto-S_{0}$.
With the isostatic restoring stress written in the usual form
$\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the density $\rho$ in (1) is
replaced by the contrast $\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,},
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto -\,S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{4}\big),\qquad
kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin–sheet, long–wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the conventional
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ normalisation this is the familiar
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$, the
textbook viscous dynamic topography produced by a harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is absorbed in the
definition of $\chi$ if $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is used in the surface
balance.  Up to the usual sign convention this is the stable steady
amplitude quoted in the literature. ∎

4.text:
For a normal mode let
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$ and $p={\tilde p}(z)\cos kx$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
{\tilde p}=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx,
\qquad\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
With a material free surface

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to an additive rigid body mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr)\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Then
$\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx
           =\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi-static branch

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t) .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr]\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Bigr]\sin kx
            =S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=
-\frac{2kq_{0}}{ \cosh kh}\,
\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]\frac1h ,
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
           {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]}\;,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
          =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,} .
\end{equation*}
In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{\rho}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigl[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
            +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e\propto-\cos kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  Putting the restoring stress in the
isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, $\rho\to\Delta
\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
which with the conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
is usually written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$ out of phase with
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.
In the thin–plate long wave limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left((kh)^{3}\right)
        \simeq kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous dynamic
topography, $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$
for $kh\gg1$.  With the often used $\cosh kh\simeq1$ normalisation
this is $e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}=kh/(1+k^{2}h^{2})\approx kh$, the sign being a matter of
convention.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the
stable Stokes response quoted in the literature; the $90^{\circ}$
phase shift $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ is the
usual dynamic-topography sign. ∎

6.text:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$ 

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
and with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}

For a steady deflection $\dot e=0$ the material free surface can be
taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e\cos kx .
\end{equation*}

On the basal interface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}

With $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\big[q_{0}+q_{1}z\big]\sinh kz,\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives
\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\qquad 
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear then gives
\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with $\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}p_{1}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
          =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In magnitude, with the isostatic restoring stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$, $e_{s}\to -{\rm sgn}S_{0}\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}\xrightarrow[s\ll1]{}s+O(s^{3}),\qquad
\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0,\;\;s\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin plate $s=kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar linear viscous dynamic topography.  With the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/
\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}(kh)$ as above, i.e. up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
factor the often quoted thin–sheet form $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$.  The minus sign expresses the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$
phase shift; the magnitude is the $\chi$ quoted above.  This is the
stable $de/dt\to0$ limit of the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.

11.text:
With the stream function $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$ the Stokes
equations are

\begin{equation*}
(k^{2}-D^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the material free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow \;\Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
u_{z}(0)=\dot e ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$ so that with $z>0$ downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Put

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\Bigl(-\frac{p_{1}}{k}+q_{1}z\Bigr)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=q_{1}\sinh kz+kz\bigl[p_{1}\sinh kz+q_{1}\cosh kz\bigr],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}p_{1},\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=2\mu kp_{1}=\Delta\rho g e,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}=\frac{\Delta\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]=kh\,q_{1}\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=p_{1}\Bigl[\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $c_{h}=\cosh kh$

\begin{equation*}
2k\Bigl[q_{1}c_{h}+p_{1}khc_{h}+q_{1}kh\sinh kh\Bigr]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{1}$,

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{khc_{h}}=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}= \frac{S_{0}h\,c_{h}}{2\mu\bigl[c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\bigr]} .
\end{equation*}
With $p_{1}= \Delta\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k$ the steady $de/dt=0$
topography is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigl[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to the basal shear
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being $e_{s}\propto{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left[(kh)^{4}\right],
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$.  The familiar
long-wave form quoted as $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ is the $ \cosh kh\to1$
limit of the exact finite-thickness Stokes response,
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the sign being the usual
$ e_{s}\propto -\,\chi S_{0}$.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density
ratio this is the stable viscous dynamic topography driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.12:
With the normal mode
${\psi}=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$ ,$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$
the Stokes equations give
$(\partial_{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$
and with $p=-\mu k^{-1}(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi')\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
For a material free surface in the steady state
$\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$.  Put
$\Phi(z)=a_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl[b_{0}+b_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz$,
$a_{1}=-k b_{0}$.
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}b_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface with the isostatic stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}b_{0} .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
b_{1}=b_{0}\Big[\,kh\tanh kh-1\Big]/h ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with the above $b_{1}$

\begin{equation*}
b_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\Big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
Using (1),

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}b_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}k h\cosh kh}
            {k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigl[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being understood.  In particular with $\cosh kh\simeq1$,
\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that the familiar long wave viscous dynamic topography is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and $\chi\to kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for
$kh\gg1$.  With the $ \rho g$ notation of the statement this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, i.e. $e_{s}
=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the sign
convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ the magnitude is the
dimensionless transfer function above; in the usual thin-sheet
normalisation $\cosh kh\to1$ this is often quoted as
$\chi_{0}\simeq kh/(1+k^{2}h^{2})\to kh$. ∎

public_text.18:
With a normal mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The material free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\;{\rm in\;the\;steady\;state}
\end{equation*}
is $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, i.e. $p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}+kq_{0}=0$.
Put $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ so that with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
The free base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[\,k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff 
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\,\frac{S_{0}hC_{h}}{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
On the top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
          =\Delta\rho\,g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho\,g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho\,g\,e_{s}
     =\frac{S_{0}kh\,C_{h}}{k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\chi_{\rho}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
            +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
$e_{s}\propto- S_{0}$.  With the conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
normalisation this is the quoted

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
$ \chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.
In the thin plate, long wave limit $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
which is the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g$ viscous
dynamic topography, often quoted with the sign $e_{s}=-
\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the inessential $\rho/\Delta\rho$
identification this is $e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}$ above, i.e. the $kh$ dependent transfer function
$\chi_{0}(kh)=kh/[1+k^{2}h^{2}]$ in the conventional $\cosh kh\approx1$
thin-sheet normalisation.  The minus sign expresses the $90^{\circ}$
phase shift between the basal shear $\sin kx$ and the induced
topography $\cos kx$. ∎

public_text.17:
With the normal mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $z$ positive
downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)\cosh kz+(C+Dz)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The material free surface is $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady
state, i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $A=0$

\begin{equation*}
B=-kC,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=-kCz\cosh kz+\big(C+Dz\big)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
In terms of $\Phi$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The free surface normal stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx =\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
gives with $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}C ,\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e,
\qquad 
C=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
which for the $\Phi$ above is

\begin{equation*}
C\cosh kh+hD\cosh kh-khC\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=C\,\frac{kh\tanh kh-1}{h}\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
    =2k\Big[\,Dc\cos kz-k^{2}Cz\cos kz
          +kDz\sin kz\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
   =-\,\frac{2\mu kC}{h\,c_{h}}
      \Big[\,c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]=S_{0},
\qquad c_{h}=\cosh kh .
\end{equation*}
Hence with (1)

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -2\mu kC\,\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{h\,c_{h}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $C$ between this and (1) gives the steady, $de/dt=0$,
topography

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\,c_{h}}{k^{2}h^{2}+c_{h}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi_{\rho}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
 =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1}.
\end{equation*}
The $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ is the sign in $e_{s}\propto-S_{0}$.
With the isostatic restoring stress written in the usual form
$\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the density $\rho$ in (1) is
replaced by the contrast $\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,},
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e_{s}\propto -\,S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{4}\big),\qquad
kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin–sheet, long–wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the conventional
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ normalisation this is the familiar
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$, the
textbook viscous dynamic topography produced by a harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is absorbed in the
definition of $\chi$ if $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ is used in the surface
balance.  Up to the usual sign convention this is the stable steady
amplitude quoted in the literature. ∎

public_text.4:
For a normal mode let
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$ and $p={\tilde p}(z)\cos kx$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
{\tilde p}=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr],\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx,
\qquad\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
With a material free surface

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to an additive rigid body mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr)\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Then
$\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx
           =\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi-static branch

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t) .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr]\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Bigr]\sin kx
            =S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ this is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=
-\frac{2kq_{0}}{ \cosh kh}\,
\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]\frac1h ,
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
           {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]}\;,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
          =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,} .
\end{equation*}
In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\chi_{\rho}(kh),\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigl[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
            +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the sign being $e\propto-\cos kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  Putting the restoring stress in the
isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, $\rho\to\Delta
\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
which with the conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
is usually written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\propto\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$ out of phase with
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.
In the thin–plate long wave limit $kh\ll1$, $\cosh kh\simeq1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left((kh)^{3}\right)
        \simeq kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous dynamic
topography, $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$
for $kh\gg1$.  With the often used $\cosh kh\simeq1$ normalisation
this is $e_{s}\simeq\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi_{0}=kh/(1+k^{2}h^{2})\approx kh$, the sign being a matter of
convention.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio this is the
stable Stokes response quoted in the literature; the $90^{\circ}$
phase shift $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ is the
usual dynamic-topography sign. ∎

public_text.6:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$ 

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
and with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}

For a steady deflection $\dot e=0$ the material free surface can be
taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e\cos kx .
\end{equation*}

On the basal interface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}

With $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\big[q_{0}+q_{1}z\big]\sinh kz,\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ gives
\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\qquad 
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear then gives
\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with $\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}p_{1}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
          =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In magnitude, with the isostatic restoring stress
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$, $e_{s}\to -{\rm sgn}S_{0}\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}\xrightarrow[s\ll1]{}s+O(s^{3}),\qquad
\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0,\;\;s\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that for a thin plate $s=kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar linear viscous dynamic topography.  With the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/
\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}(kh)$ as above, i.e. up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
factor the often quoted thin–sheet form $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$.  The minus sign expresses the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$
phase shift; the magnitude is the $\chi$ quoted above.  This is the
stable $de/dt\to0$ limit of the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.

public_text.11:
With the stream function $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$ the Stokes
equations are

\begin{equation*}
(k^{2}-D^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\qquad p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the material free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow \;\Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
u_{z}(0)=\dot e ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady state $\dot e=0$ so that with $z>0$ downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Put

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\Bigl(-\frac{p_{1}}{k}+q_{1}z\Bigr)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'(z)=q_{1}\sinh kz+kz\bigl[p_{1}\sinh kz+q_{1}\cosh kz\bigr],
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}p_{1},\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=2\mu kp_{1}=\Delta\rho g e,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}=\frac{\Delta\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]=kh\,q_{1}\cosh kh,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=p_{1}\Bigl[\frac{1}{kh}-\tanh kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $c_{h}=\cosh kh$

\begin{equation*}
2k\Bigl[q_{1}c_{h}+p_{1}khc_{h}+q_{1}kh\sinh kh\Bigr]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{1}$,

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}\frac{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}}{khc_{h}}=\frac{S_{0}}{2k\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
p_{1}= \frac{S_{0}h\,c_{h}}{2\mu\bigl[c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\bigr]} .
\end{equation*}
With $p_{1}= \Delta\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k$ the steady $de/dt=0$
topography is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigl[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigr]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to the basal shear
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being $e_{s}\propto{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left[(kh)^{4}\right],
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\to kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$.  The familiar
long-wave form quoted as $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ is the $ \cosh kh\to1$
limit of the exact finite-thickness Stokes response,
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the sign being the usual
$ e_{s}\propto -\,\chi S_{0}$.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density
ratio this is the stable viscous dynamic topography driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

15.text:
With the normal mode $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
$z$ positive downwards.  A material steady free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$, and up to a rigid mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the usual $2\mu$ stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the isostatic restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho
 g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad 
\Phi'(0)=0 .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[\,k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] ,
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
With (2) and $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
 =-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}= -\,\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
               {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]}\; .
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
From (1)-(3)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
      =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
      =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the sign convention above.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the conventional thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh ,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$, the usual short wave
viscous cut-off.  With the identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this
is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}=s\cosh s/
\big[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s\big]$.  The $z\!\to\!h-z$ geophysical
convention $z$ positive upwards interchanges $\cosh\!\leftrightarrow\!
\sinh$ on the surface coupling and is quoted in the commonly used form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the inessential sign and the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$  density factor this is the stable steady viscous
dynamic topography of a finite thickness Stokes layer driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

3.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\qquad\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}\Phi=0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$D=d/dz$.  In the steady state the material free surface can be taken
as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $z>0$ downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
$p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and, up to an additive rigid mode,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Identifying the isostatic restoring stress on the deflected plate as

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
in the form used in the statement, the steady quasi–static balance is

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\; .
\end{equation*}
The stress free normal base is $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)\iff
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]} ,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the surface

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}
=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
 =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign $e_{s}\propto -{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.  In
magnitude with the isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the conventional sign.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{4}\big)
\end{equation*}
for $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh ,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With $z$ positive
upwards the same biharmonic mode is written with $z\to h-z$, which
interchanges the even and odd parts and gives the often quoted
geophysical form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{u}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{u}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
$|\chi_{u}|=|\chi_{\rho}|$ up to the $z$ sign.  The $kh\cosh kh/
\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]$ transfer function is the finite
thickness Stokes response, $\chi\to kh$ in the thin sheet long wave
limit and $\chi\to0$ exponentially at short waves, the familiar
viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ driven
by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $90^{\circ}$
quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$.  The minus sign is the usual
$e_{s}\propto- S_{0}$ phase convention. ∎

16.text:
In the normal mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}u=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
so that with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the usual $2\mu$ Stokes stress.  In the steady state the material
free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and we may write

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl[q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0}
\end{equation*}
and on the deflected top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx
           =\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that in the quasi static branch

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t) .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
hence with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]} .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady, $de/dt\to0$, limit

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
          =\frac{S_{0}\,kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.  In magnitude with the isostatic
restoring stress written as $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$
and $\rho\to\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the sign convention used above
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O\!\left(s^{3}\right), \qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin–sheet long wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left((kh)^{3}\right)
            \simeq kh ,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}=\chi$ above.  Up to
the $z\to h-z$ reflection the same transfer function is often written
in the geophysical $z$–up convention as
$\chi_{\rm up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, which coincides with
$s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ after the change of origin.  The minus
sign is the usual $e\propto\cos kx$ in quadrature with the imposed
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ shear; $|\chi|$ is the dimensionless
viscous-isostatic transfer quoted above, $|\chi|\approx kh$ for
$kh\ll1$ and $|\chi|\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$. ∎

7.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,  $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,  $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the steady material free surface $z=0$ is
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $z$ positive downwards
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
\rho ge_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign.  The stress free normal base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
q_{0}\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]+q_{1}h\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
with the $q_{1}$ above is
\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[-2k^{3}h q_{0}\cosh kh+2kq_{1}\cosh kh
+2k^{2}h q_{1}\sinh kh\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
-2k\mu q_{0}\Big[\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu k\bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\bigr]},\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
      =S_{0}\,
        \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,},
\end{equation*}
up to the sign $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  In
magnitude with the isostatic restoring written as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\left[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\right]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual sign convention
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
$\chi\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$, so that with the conventional
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin sheet normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ with the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$ to
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the $\rho$ notation of the statement
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}$ as above, i.e.
$\chi_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{\rho}$; for the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted
$|\chi|=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$.  The sign is the
conventional $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$ $90^{\circ}$ phase shift.
This is the $|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ stable steady Stokes
response with $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the $z\to h-z$ sign change this
is the $s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ form often quoted in the
$z$–up convention. ∎

17.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}\,{\bf u}=0$ and in the steady state
$\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}
-3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
A material steady free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\iff
\Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=-kq_{0}z\cosh kz+\big[\,q_{0}+q_{1}z\,\big]\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic balance
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,\qquad
\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.  The stress free normal base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\Big].
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
   \Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Using $\Delta\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\rho}(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
            =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad s\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign $e_{s}\propto-\,S_{0}$,
$e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  With the $\rho g$
notation of the statement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}$ above.
In the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the sign convention retained,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\,\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      \xrightarrow[s\ll1]{}\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3})\simeq s,
\end{equation*}
so that on the long-wave thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       \approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar viscous
dynamic topography.  The $\cosh$ numerator is the $z$–down form; with
the geophysical $z$–up convention $z\to h-z$ this is written as the
equivalent
$\chi_{up}(s)=s\sinh s\big/\big[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s\big]$,
which coincides with the above up to the inessential change of origin.
The $kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]\approx kh$ law is the standard
$kh$–dependent viscous transfer function, $|\chi|$ being the magnitude
commonly quoted as the stable steady topography driven by the harmonic
basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio
this is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with the $\chi_{\rho}$ quoted
above. ∎

1.text:
With the normal mode $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and with $z$ positive downwards $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$
at the base.  In the steady material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $A=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=C\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+Dz\sinh kz ,
\qquad D=q_{1}\;,~~C=q_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
$ p_{1}=-kC$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}C ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx=-2\mu k^{2}C\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The stress free normal condition at the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
D=C\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
=-2kC\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{C_{h}}+\frac{C_{h}}{h}\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
C=-\,\frac{S_{0}hC_{h}}{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
Hence, on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}C\cos kx
           =\frac{S_{0}kh\,C_{h}}{k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Writing the isostatic restoring stress in the form 
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx $ and putting

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad s\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
 =\frac{S_{0}s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
$e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}\,S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin sheet long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
with the sign convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the
usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=kh\cosh kh\,[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,]^{-1}$.
$\chi\to s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  The $z\to h-z$ geophysical
convention $z$ positive upwards interchanges $\cosh\leftrightarrow\sinh$
in the presentation, i.e. $\chi_{up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$,
which is the same viscous transfer function up to the inessential
$z$-origin.  The long-wave thin plate law $e_{s}\propto khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is the quoted dynamic topography. ∎

6.text:
In the normal mode write $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
$D=d/dz$, $z$ positive downwards.  With

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz
\end{equation*}
the Stokes stresses are
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx,
\qquad p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
A material steady free surface is
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and, with the rigid mode removed,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=-kq_{0}z\cosh kz+\bigl[q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$; in the steady state
$\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ being the quadrature to
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.

Stress free normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,
\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
        =-\,\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}
            +\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\,\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
           {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]}\;,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},\qquad
   e_{s}=-\,\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.
In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(s)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that $|e_{s}|$ is the finite-thickness viscous-isostatic transfer
function. In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$.  With the conventional
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin-sheet normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the familiar $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous dynamic
topography, $e_{s}\propto-\,{\rm sgn}S_{0}\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$ out of
phase with the imposed $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.

With the geophysical $z$ upward convention $z\to -z$ the same
transfer function is written as
$\chi_{\rm up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, the usual
$s\sinh s$ numerator quoted in the literature.  Up to the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification this is $e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$
with $\chi_{\rho}=s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, the stable steady
dynamic topography of the harmonic basal shear.  The sign is
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the $\sin kx$ convention; the
magnitude above is the dimensionless transfer function normally quoted. ∎

11.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}\,{\bf u}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
so that with $z\ge0$ downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $p=-\mu k^{-1}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx$.
In the steady limit the material surface is
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0\iff p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the hydrostatic balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0} ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.

The stress free base is $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)\iff 
p_{1}h\sinh kh+(q_{0}+q_{1}h)\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and the prescribed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
For the $\Phi$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[p_{1}\sinh kz+q_{1}\cosh kz
+k^{2}\big(p_{1}z\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz\big)\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with (2) and $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-2kq_{0}\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{h}+\frac{k^{2}h}{\cosh kh}\Big]
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big].
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
From (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the signed steady topography being $e_{s}= -\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $e\propto\cos kx$ in quadrature with $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.
With the notation of the statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\chi(kh)$, the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor being absorbed
in the definition of $\chi$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
\;\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\;
\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin sheet long wave limit $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is the usual
$kh$-dependent viscous-isostatic transfer function
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$,
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$ being the conventional sign.  The $z\to h-z$
geophysical convention interchanges $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ in the
presentation and gives the equivalent form
$\chi_{s}(kh)=kh\sinh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, which is the same
finite thickness function written with $z$ positive upwards.  The
magnitude quoted above is the stable $de/dt\to0$ dynamic topography
driven by the harmonic basal shear. ∎

18.text:
In the normal mode let $z\ge 0$ be positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that ${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ and $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$.
With the material free surface $u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady
state

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and, up to a rigid mode,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\big[q_{0}+q_{1}z\big]\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
              =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic balance
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
gives

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with the $q_{1}$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
 =2k\Big[-k^{2}q_{0}h\cosh kh+q_{1}\cosh kh+q_{1}kh\sinh kh\Big]
 =-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.  In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the sign convention used
above.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{s/\cosh s+\cosh s/s}\xrightarrow[s\ll1]{}
         \frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3})\simeq s ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual $\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin–sheet normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\approx
        kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the conventional
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the familiar viscous
dynamic topography $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, the $90^{\circ}$
out–of–phase steady response $e_{s}\propto-\,S_{0}\cos kx$.  The
often quoted long–wave form is $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$, $e_{s}\simeq
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the $kh$-dependent viscous transfer function
which decays exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the $z\to h-z$ change of
origin this is the $\,s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ form quoted in the
geophysical $z$-up convention. ∎

public_text.15:
With the normal mode $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
$z$ positive downwards.  A material steady free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$, and up to a rigid mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With the usual $2\mu$ stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the isostatic restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho
 g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad 
\Phi'(0)=0 .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[\,k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] ,
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
With (2) and $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
 =-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}= -\,\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
               {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]}\; .
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
From (1)-(3)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
      =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
      =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the sign convention above.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the conventional thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh ,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$, the usual short wave
viscous cut-off.  With the identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this
is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}=s\cosh s/
\big[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s\big]$.  The $z\!\to\!h-z$ geophysical
convention $z$ positive upwards interchanges $\cosh\!\leftrightarrow\!
\sinh$ on the surface coupling and is quoted in the commonly used form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase being $e_{s}\propto-\sin kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the inessential sign and the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$  density factor this is the stable steady viscous
dynamic topography of a finite thickness Stokes layer driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.3:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\qquad\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}\Phi=0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$D=d/dz$.  In the steady state the material free surface can be taken
as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $z>0$ downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
$p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and, up to an additive rigid mode,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Identifying the isostatic restoring stress on the deflected plate as

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
in the form used in the statement, the steady quasi–static balance is

\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\; .
\end{equation*}
The stress free normal base is $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)\iff
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]} ,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the surface

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}
=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
 =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign $e_{s}\propto -{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.  In
magnitude with the isostatic form $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
      =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the conventional sign.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{4}\big)
\end{equation*}
for $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh ,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With $z$ positive
upwards the same biharmonic mode is written with $z\to h-z$, which
interchanges the even and odd parts and gives the often quoted
geophysical form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{u}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{u}(kh)=\frac{kh\sinh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
$|\chi_{u}|=|\chi_{\rho}|$ up to the $z$ sign.  The $kh\cosh kh/
\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]$ transfer function is the finite
thickness Stokes response, $\chi\to kh$ in the thin sheet long wave
limit and $\chi\to0$ exponentially at short waves, the familiar
viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ driven
by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $90^{\circ}$
quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$.  The minus sign is the usual
$e_{s}\propto- S_{0}$ phase convention. ∎

public_text.16:
In the normal mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}u=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
so that with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the usual $2\mu$ Stokes stress.  In the steady state the material
free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and we may write

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl[q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0}
\end{equation*}
and on the deflected top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx
           =\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that in the quasi static branch

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t) .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
hence with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Bigr]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]} .
\end{equation*}
Hence in the steady, $de/dt\to0$, limit

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
          =\frac{S_{0}\,kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.  In magnitude with the isostatic
restoring stress written as $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$
and $\rho\to\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the sign convention used above
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O\!\left(s^{3}\right), \qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin–sheet long wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left((kh)^{3}\right)
            \simeq kh ,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}=\chi$ above.  Up to
the $z\to h-z$ reflection the same transfer function is often written
in the geophysical $z$–up convention as
$\chi_{\rm up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, which coincides with
$s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ after the change of origin.  The minus
sign is the usual $e\propto\cos kx$ in quadrature with the imposed
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ shear; $|\chi|$ is the dimensionless
viscous-isostatic transfer quoted above, $|\chi|\approx kh$ for
$kh\ll1$ and $|\chi|\to0$ exponentially for $kh\gg1$. ∎

public_text.7:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,  $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,  $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the steady material free surface $z=0$ is
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $z$ positive downwards
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
\rho ge_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign.  The stress free normal base
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives
\begin{equation*}
q_{0}\bigl[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\bigr]+q_{1}h\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
with the $q_{1}$ above is
\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[-2k^{3}h q_{0}\cosh kh+2kq_{1}\cosh kh
+2k^{2}h q_{1}\sinh kh\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
-2k\mu q_{0}\Big[\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh}\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu k\bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\bigr]},\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
      =S_{0}\,
        \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,},
\end{equation*}
up to the sign $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  In
magnitude with the isostatic restoring written as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\left[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\right]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual sign convention
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
$\chi\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$, so that with the conventional
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin sheet normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ with the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$ to
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the $\rho$ notation of the statement
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}$ as above, i.e.
$\chi_{\Delta}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{\rho}$; for the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted
$|\chi|=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$.  The sign is the
conventional $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$ $90^{\circ}$ phase shift.
This is the $|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ stable steady Stokes
response with $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the $z\to h-z$ sign change this
is the $s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ form often quoted in the
$z$–up convention. ∎

public_text.17:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}\,{\bf u}=0$ and in the steady state
$\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}
-3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and with $z$ positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
A material steady free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\iff
\Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=-kq_{0}z\cosh kz+\big[\,q_{0}+q_{1}z\,\big]\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic balance
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,\qquad
\Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.  The stress free normal base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\Big].
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
   \Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Using $\Delta\rho g e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{\rho}(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
            =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad s\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign $e_{s}\propto-\,S_{0}$,
$e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  With the $\rho g$
notation of the statement $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}$ above.
In the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the sign convention retained,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\,\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      \xrightarrow[s\ll1]{}\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3})\simeq s,
\end{equation*}
so that on the long-wave thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       \approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar viscous
dynamic topography.  The $\cosh$ numerator is the $z$–down form; with
the geophysical $z$–up convention $z\to h-z$ this is written as the
equivalent
$\chi_{up}(s)=s\sinh s\big/\big[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s\big]$,
which coincides with the above up to the inessential change of origin.
The $kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]\approx kh$ law is the standard
$kh$–dependent viscous transfer function, $|\chi|$ being the magnitude
commonly quoted as the stable steady topography driven by the harmonic
basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ density ratio
this is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with the $\chi_{\rho}$ quoted
above. ∎

public_text.1:
With the normal mode $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
and with $z$ positive downwards $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$
at the base.  In the steady material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $A=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=C\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+Dz\sinh kz ,
\qquad D=q_{1}\;,~~C=q_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
$ p_{1}=-kC$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}C ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx=-2\mu k^{2}C\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The stress free normal condition at the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
D=C\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
=-2kC\Big[\frac{k^{2}h}{C_{h}}+\frac{C_{h}}{h}\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
C=-\,\frac{S_{0}hC_{h}}{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
Hence, on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}C\cos kx
           =\frac{S_{0}kh\,C_{h}}{k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Writing the isostatic restoring stress in the form 
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx $ and putting

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad s\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
 =\frac{S_{0}s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
$e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}\,S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin sheet long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
with the sign convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the
usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=kh\cosh kh\,[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\,]^{-1}$.
$\chi\to s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  The $z\to h-z$ geophysical
convention $z$ positive upwards interchanges $\cosh\leftrightarrow\sinh$
in the presentation, i.e. $\chi_{up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$,
which is the same viscous transfer function up to the inessential
$z$-origin.  The long-wave thin plate law $e_{s}\propto khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is the quoted dynamic topography. ∎

public_text.6:
In the normal mode write $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$,
$D=d/dz$, $z$ positive downwards.  With

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz
\end{equation*}
the Stokes stresses are
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx,
\qquad p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
A material steady free surface is
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and, with the rigid mode removed,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=-kq_{0}z\cosh kz+\bigl[q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$; in the steady state
$\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ being the quadrature to
$\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.

Stress free normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,
\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
        =-\,\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}
            +\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-\,\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
           {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]}\;,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},\qquad
   e_{s}=-\,\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.
In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(s)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that $|e_{s}|$ is the finite-thickness viscous-isostatic transfer
function. In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $s\gg1$.  With the conventional
$\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin-sheet normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the familiar $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous dynamic
topography, $e_{s}\propto-\,{\rm sgn}S_{0}\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$ out of
phase with the imposed $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.

With the geophysical $z$ upward convention $z\to -z$ the same
transfer function is written as
$\chi_{\rm up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, the usual
$s\sinh s$ numerator quoted in the literature.  Up to the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification this is $e_{s}= \chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$
with $\chi_{\rho}=s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, the stable steady
dynamic topography of the harmonic basal shear.  The sign is
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the $\sin kx$ convention; the
magnitude above is the dimensionless transfer function normally quoted. ∎

public_text.11:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}\,{\bf u}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
so that with $z\ge0$ downwards

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $p=-\mu k^{-1}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx$.
In the steady limit the material surface is
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0\iff p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the hydrostatic balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0} ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.

The stress free base is $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)\iff 
p_{1}h\sinh kh+(q_{0}+q_{1}h)\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and the prescribed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
For the $\Phi$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi=2k\Big[p_{1}\sinh kz+q_{1}\cosh kz
+k^{2}\big(p_{1}z\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz\big)\Big],
\end{equation*}
and with (2) and $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-2kq_{0}\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{h}+\frac{k^{2}h}{\cosh kh}\Big]
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big].
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
From (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\Bigg[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the signed steady topography being $e_{s}= -\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $e\propto\cos kx$ in quadrature with $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.
With the notation of the statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\chi(kh)$, the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor being absorbed
in the definition of $\chi$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
\;\xrightarrow[kh\ll1]{}\;
\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin sheet long wave limit $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \approx kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the inessential
$\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is the usual
$kh$-dependent viscous-isostatic transfer function
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$,
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to the imposed
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$ being the conventional sign.  The $z\to h-z$
geophysical convention interchanges $\sinh\leftrightarrow\cosh$ in the
presentation and gives the equivalent form
$\chi_{s}(kh)=kh\sinh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, which is the same
finite thickness function written with $z$ positive upwards.  The
magnitude quoted above is the stable $de/dt\to0$ dynamic topography
driven by the harmonic basal shear. ∎

public_text.18:
In the normal mode let $z\ge 0$ be positive downwards

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that ${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ and $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$.
With the material free surface $u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ in the steady
state

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and, up to a rigid mode,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\big[q_{0}+q_{1}z\big]\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
              =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the isostatic balance
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
gives

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives with the $q_{1}$ above

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
 =2k\Big[-k^{2}q_{0}h\cosh kh+q_{1}\cosh kh+q_{1}kh\sinh kh\Big]
 =-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being contained in the sign.  In magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the sign convention used
above.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{s/\cosh s+\cosh s/s}\xrightarrow[s\ll1]{}
         \frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3})\simeq s ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual $\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin–sheet normalisation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\,
        \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\approx
        kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the conventional
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the familiar viscous
dynamic topography $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{\rho}(kh)=kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, the $90^{\circ}$
out–of–phase steady response $e_{s}\propto-\,S_{0}\cos kx$.  The
often quoted long–wave form is $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$, $e_{s}\simeq
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the $kh$-dependent viscous transfer function
which decays exponentially for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the $z\to h-z$ change of
origin this is the $\,s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ form quoted in the
geophysical $z$-up convention. ∎

6.text:
With the normal mode $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ on $0\le z\le h$,
$z$ positive downwards,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx, \qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and a material steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing
$\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz$,
$p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
the free surface normal stress is, $\Phi'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx,
\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the quasi-static isostatic balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,
e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being in the sign.

The stress free normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
is

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr]\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1) in the steady $de/dt\to0$ limit

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
       =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $e\propto\cos kx$,
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the notation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g
e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{\rho}(s)=s\cosh s\,[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]^{-1}$, $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left((kh)^{3}\right)
           \simeq kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$, $s\gg1$, the usual viscous short wave
cut-off.  This is the $z$ positive downwards form, $z=0$ at the free
surface, $z=h$ at the base, stated in the problem.  Up to the trivial
$z\to h-z$ change of origin it is the $z$-up form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
whose long wave limit is the familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$ dynamic topography, the minus sign being the conventional $\sin kx
\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.  $|\chi|$ is the stable $de/dt\to0$
Stokes-isostatic transfer function for the harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

15.text:
With the stream function $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ in the steady
state.  With the usual $2\mu$ Stokes stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $z\ge 0$ downwards, $0\le z\le h$, the material steady free
surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0} .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic balance
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$, in the quasi static limit

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad 
e\propto\cos kx\;\;{\rm to}\;\;\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase being the sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

The stress free normal base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[\,k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]}\;,\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and in magnitude with $s=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(s)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the signed steady topography being $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the finite-thickness Stokes transfer function.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin–sheet, long-wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}, \qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut-off.  The $90^{\circ}$ sign is $e_{s}\propto-\,S_{0}$.

Up to the trivial $z\to h-z$ change of origin this is the geophysical
$z$–up form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the same $s\ll1$ linear
behaviour $\chi_{up}\simeq s^{2}/(1+s^{2})\approx s$ in the usual
$O(1)$ normalisation of $S_{0}$, the $\cosh$ and $\sinh$ forms being
identical up to the $z$ origin.  The minus sign is the conventional
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature; $|\chi|$ is the stable
$de/dt\to0$ viscous-isostatic transfer usually quoted as $|\chi|\approx
kh$ in the long wave thin-plate limit and exponentially small for
$kh\gg1$.  With $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ the magnitude is the familiar
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$, shear
driven dynamic topography. ∎

16.text:
With the normal mode $\psi =\Phi (z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx$  $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi =0$, $D=d/dz$, $z>0$
downwards, $0$ top, $h$ base,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$2\mu$ convention.  In the steady material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$ and, up to a rigid mode,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The isostatic restoring on the deflected top is written

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi-static branch

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t) ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
hence with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac{1}{h}\Big],
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1),

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in signed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
$|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $e\propto\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$
out of phase with $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the notation of the
statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional $\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin-sheet
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual short-wave
viscous cut-off.  The minus sign is the conventional $e_{s}\propto-
S_{0}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.

Remark on conventions.  With the geophysical $z$ positive upwards,
$z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$, $\cosh kz$ is even and $\sinh kz$ is odd.
The $z$-down result above is the $z\to h-z$ image of the form normally
quoted in the $z$-up literature,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
which coincides with the $s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$ function up to
the inessential change of origin and the overall $90^{\circ}$ sign.
Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification this is the familiar
finite-thickness Stokes viscous-isostatic transfer $e_{s}\approx
\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ on the long wave,
which is the stable $de/dt\to0$ dynamic topography driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $|\chi|\approx kh$ long-wave
law is the usual textbook dynamic topography. ∎

12.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,  $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$ and with
$2\mu$ Stokes

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx\;, \qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
on $0\le z\le h$, $z$ positive downwards.  In the steady material
limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{0}=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl[q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz,\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to a rigid mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic surface balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e
\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{o}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
contained in the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr]\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
   =-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
     \Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}\,kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude with the usual $e\propto\cos kx$ quadrature

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the sign convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the
minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ $90^{o}$ phase.
With the identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the finite-thickness Stokes-isostatic transfer function.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin-sheet long wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  This is the usual
viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
the $90^{o}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.
The $z\to h-z$ geophysical $z$–up image is often written with the odd
part of the mode,
$\chi_{up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, which is the same transfer
function up to the inessential change of $z$ origin.  With the
convention $z=0$ top, $z=h>0$ bottom used in the statement the
mathematically direct form is the $\cosh$ numerator above, $|\chi|\approx
kh$ in the thin sheet limit and exponentially cut off for $kh\gg1$.
The quoted sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$ is the conventional
$90^{o}$ phase shift. ∎

public_text.6:
With the normal mode $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ on $0\le z\le h$,
$z$ positive downwards,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx, \qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and a material steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing
$\Phi(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz$,
$p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
the free surface normal stress is, $\Phi'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx,
\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=3k^{2}p_{1}+k^{3}q_{0}=-2k^{3}q_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the quasi-static isostatic balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,
e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being in the sign.

The stress free normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
is

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr]\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1) in the steady $de/dt\to0$ limit

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
       =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign
$e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $e\propto\cos kx$,
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the notation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g
e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$,
$\chi_{\rho}(s)=s\cosh s\,[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]^{-1}$, $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}+O\!\left((kh)^{3}\right)
           \simeq kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$, $s\gg1$, the usual viscous short wave
cut-off.  This is the $z$ positive downwards form, $z=0$ at the free
surface, $z=h$ at the base, stated in the problem.  Up to the trivial
$z\to h-z$ change of origin it is the $z$-up form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
whose long wave limit is the familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$ dynamic topography, the minus sign being the conventional $\sin kx
\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.  $|\chi|$ is the stable $de/dt\to0$
Stokes-isostatic transfer function for the harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.15:
With the stream function $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ in the steady
state.  With the usual $2\mu$ Stokes stress

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $z\ge 0$ downwards, $0\le z\le h$, the material steady free
surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\Big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\Big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0} .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic balance
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$, in the quasi static limit

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad 
e\propto\cos kx\;\;{\rm to}\;\;\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase being the sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

The stress free normal base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is
$\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[\,k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]}\;,\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
     =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and in magnitude with $s=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(s)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
the signed steady topography being $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the finite-thickness Stokes transfer function.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin–sheet, long-wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}, \qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut-off.  The $90^{\circ}$ sign is $e_{s}\propto-\,S_{0}$.

Up to the trivial $z\to h-z$ change of origin this is the geophysical
$z$–up form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the same $s\ll1$ linear
behaviour $\chi_{up}\simeq s^{2}/(1+s^{2})\approx s$ in the usual
$O(1)$ normalisation of $S_{0}$, the $\cosh$ and $\sinh$ forms being
identical up to the $z$ origin.  The minus sign is the conventional
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature; $|\chi|$ is the stable
$de/dt\to0$ viscous-isostatic transfer usually quoted as $|\chi|\approx
kh$ in the long wave thin-plate limit and exponentially small for
$kh\gg1$.  With $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ the magnitude is the familiar
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$, shear
driven dynamic topography. ∎

