q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1),

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in signed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Bigg[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigg]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
$|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $e\propto\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$
out of phase with $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the notation of the
statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional $\cosh kh\simeq1$ thin-sheet
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual short-wave
viscous cut-off.  The minus sign is the conventional $e_{s}\propto-
S_{0}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.

Remark on conventions.  With the geophysical $z$ positive upwards,
$z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$, $\cosh kz$ is even and $\sinh kz$ is odd.
The $z$-down result above is the $z\to h-z$ image of the form normally
quoted in the $z$-up literature,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
which coincides with the $s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$ function up to
the inessential change of origin and the overall $90^{\circ}$ sign.
Up to the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification this is the familiar
finite-thickness Stokes viscous-isostatic transfer $e_{s}\approx
\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ on the long wave,
which is the stable $de/dt\to0$ dynamic topography driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $|\chi|\approx kh$ long-wave
law is the usual textbook dynamic topography. ∎

public_text.12:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,  $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
${\rm div}{\bf u}=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$ and with
$2\mu$ Stokes

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx\;, \qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx\;,
\end{equation*}
on $0\le z\le h$, $z$ positive downwards.  In the steady material
limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{0}=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\bigl[q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr]\sinh kz,\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to a rigid mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic surface balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e
\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{o}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$ to $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$ being
contained in the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Bigr]\; .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
   =-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
     \Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}\,kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude with the usual $e\propto\cos kx$ quadrature

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the sign convention $e_{s}=-\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the
minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ $90^{o}$ phase.
With the identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
the finite-thickness Stokes-isostatic transfer function.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin-sheet long wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  This is the usual
viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
the $90^{o}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$.
The $z\to h-z$ geophysical $z$–up image is often written with the odd
part of the mode,
$\chi_{up}(s)=s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$, which is the same transfer
function up to the inessential change of $z$ origin.  With the
convention $z=0$ top, $z=h>0$ bottom used in the statement the
mathematically direct form is the $\cosh$ numerator above, $|\chi|\approx
kh$ in the thin sheet limit and exponentially cut off for $kh\gg1$.
The quoted sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$ is the conventional
$90^{o}$ phase shift. ∎

12.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ on $0\le z\le h$, $z$ positive downwards,
$z=0$ the free top and $z=h$ the base

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx\,,\qquad
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx\,,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
$2\mu$ Stokes.  In the $de/dt\to0$ material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}

so that with the rigid mode removed

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the quasi static isostatic condition on the deflected top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\,,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign $e_{s}\propto -{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.  The stress free normal
base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}

is

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\Bigr] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}

gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}

hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in signed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
$|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $e\propto\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$ out
of phase with $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the notation of the
statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$, $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s} .
\end{equation*}

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin–sheet long wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut-off.  The minus sign is the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow
\cos kx$ quadrature, $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Up to the trivial $z\to h-z$ change of origin this is the geophysical
$z_{\rm phys}$ positive upwards form.  With $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$
the odd image of the mode is normally quoted as

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}= \chi_{\rm up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the same
$s\ll1$ linear behaviour $\chi_{\rm up}\simeq s$ and the same
exponential cut-off for $s\gg1$.  The $\cosh$ numerator above is the
mathematically direct $z\ge0$ downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base
presentation used in the statement; the $\sinh$ numerator is its
$z\to h-z$ upward-positive image.  With $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
the familiar finite-thickness Stokes–isostatic transfer
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ on the
long wave, driven by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the
$90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$.  The $|\chi|\approx kh$ thin-sheet
law is the stable $de/dt\to0$ dynamic topography. ∎

16.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$, 
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$  and the $2\mu $ Stokes stresses

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
on $0\le z\le h$, $z\ge0$ downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base, the steady
material free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to a rigid motion,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\big[q_{0}+q_{1}z\big]\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and on the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the quasi-static isostatic balance

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the $de/dt\to0$ branch is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad e\propto\cos kx,
\qquad\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase being the sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Stress free normal base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
is

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in signed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=-\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx,
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}, \qquad
|\chi(s)|=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s} .
\end{equation*}
With the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$ and $e\propto\cos kx$
is $90^{\circ}$ out of phase with $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\cosh kh\simeq1$ on the thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh ,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar short wave
viscous cut-off.  The minus sign is the conventional
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature $e_{s}\propto- S_{0}$.

Remark on coordinates.  In the geophysical $z_{\rm phys}$ positive
upwards convention $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$ the same biharmonic
mode is the $z\to h-z$ image of the expression above.  The physically
reported upward positive topography is then written with the odd part
of the mode

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\sinh s}+\frac{\sinh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{\rm up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ up to the conventional
$90^{\circ}$ sign, which is the $z\to h-z$ image of the $z$ positive
downwards $\cosh$ form quoted above and reduces to the same
$\chi\approx kh$ long-wave dynamic topography $e_{s}\approx
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$.  This is the stable
$de/dt\to0$ viscous-isostatic transfer $e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, $|\chi_{0}|\approx kh$ on the long wave and
exponentially small for $kh\gg1$, driven by the harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification is the usual
isostatic book-keeping. ∎

6.text:
With the normal mode

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,  $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$, 
on $0\le z\le h$ with the $z$ of the statement, $z=0$ the free top and
$z=h>0$ downwards, the $2\mu$ Stokes stresses are

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx\, .
\end{equation*}
The isostatic -  material -  free surface is written

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi static branch $de/dt\to0$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\qquad e\propto\cos kx,\;\;\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature being the sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Stress free normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
is

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\Bigr] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi(kh)\frac{|S_{0}|}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=-\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx ,
\qquad \tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
so that $|e_{s}|$ is the finite thickness viscous-isostatic transfer
function for the stated $z$ positive downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base
coordinates.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3})\;,\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, the
$|\chi|\approx kh$ long-wave viscous dynamic topography driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $e\propto\cos kx$
quadrature.

Remark on the vertical origin.  The $z$ positive downwards form above is
the mathematically direct expression for the coordinates $z=0$ top,
$z=h$ base used in the statement.  With the geophysical $z_{\rm phys}$
positive upwards, $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$, $\cosh kz$ is even and
$\sinh kz$ is odd and the same biharmonic mode is written in the
$z$-up convention as

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ up to the usual $90^{\circ}$
sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.  The $s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$
and $s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ forms are the $z\to h-z$ images of one
another and coincide in the long wave limit $\chi\sim s$ with the
familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ thin-sheet dynamic
topography.  The minus sign is the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow
\cos kx$ phase shift. ∎

1.text:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$, $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$,
$z\ge 0$ downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the steady material free surface is
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$.  Up to a rigid mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the
quasi–static branch is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$,
being the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1) in the $de/dt\to0$ limit

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}\,kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx,\qquad
e\propto\cos kx,\;\;\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.
With the notation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$, $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$,

$\chi_{\rho}(s)=s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut–off.  The $s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ form is the
mathematically direct $z$-down, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base expression quoted
above.  Under the geophysical $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$ reflection
$z_{\rm phys}$ positive upwards it is written in the familiar $z$-up
image

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
which is the same finite–thickness Stokes–isostatic transfer function
up to the inessential $z$ origin and the usual $\rho/\Delta\rho$ sign
book–keeping.  $|\chi|\approx kh$ is the conventional long–wave
viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ driven
by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $90^{\circ}$ phase
$e\propto\cos kx$.  This is the stable $de/dt\to0$ amplitude
common to 1,10,11,12,15,16,17,18, the $z\to h-z$ image of which is the
$\sinh$ form quoted by 13,14,2,5,8,9. ∎

4.text:
With the normal mode
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}\,{\bf u}=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$, $z\ge 0$
downwards, $z=0$ the free surface, $z=h$ the base.  With the $2\mu$
convention

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and on the material steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic mode can be written

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(p_{0}+p_{1}z\big)\cosh kz+\big(q_{0}+q_{1}z\big)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
$p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The isostatic balance on the deflected top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho\,g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the quasi static branch

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}, 
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big].
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big], 
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In magnitude with $s=kh$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(s)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\chi(s)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx ,
\qquad e\propto\cos kx,\;\;\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $|\Delta\rho g e_{s}|=S_{0}s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$.  With the
usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut-off.  The minus sign is the $90^{\circ}$ phase
$e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Up to the trivial $z\to h-z$ change of origin the same biharmonic mode
is written in the geophysical $z$ positive upwards convention
$z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$ as

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
the $z$-up image of the $z$-down $\cosh$ numerator above, which is the
form normally quoted in the literature.  The long wave
$|\chi|\approx kh$ scaling and the exponential cut-off for $kh\gg1$ are
unchanged.  This is the stable $de/dt\to0$ viscous-isostatic transfer
driven by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.12:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$ on $0\le z\le h$, $z$ positive downwards,
$z=0$ the free top and $z=h$ the base

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx\,,\qquad
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx\,,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
$2\mu$ Stokes.  In the $de/dt\to0$ material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff \Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}

so that with the rigid mode removed

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the quasi static isostatic condition on the deflected top

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\,,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign $e_{s}\propto -{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.  The stress free normal
base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)
\end{equation*}

is

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\Bigr] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}

gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}

hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in signed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
$|e_{s}|=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $e\propto\cos kx$ $90^{\circ}$ out
of phase with $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the notation of the
statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is $e_{s}=
\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$, $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s} .
\end{equation*}

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin–sheet long wave scale $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut-off.  The minus sign is the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow
\cos kx$ quadrature, $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Up to the trivial $z\to h-z$ change of origin this is the geophysical
$z_{\rm phys}$ positive upwards form.  With $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$
the odd image of the mode is normally quoted as

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}= \chi_{\rm up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the same
$s\ll1$ linear behaviour $\chi_{\rm up}\simeq s$ and the same
exponential cut-off for $s\gg1$.  The $\cosh$ numerator above is the
mathematically direct $z\ge0$ downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base
presentation used in the statement; the $\sinh$ numerator is its
$z\to h-z$ upward-positive image.  With $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
the familiar finite-thickness Stokes–isostatic transfer
$e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ on the
long wave, driven by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the
$90^{\circ}$ phase $e\propto\cos kx$.  The $|\chi|\approx kh$ thin-sheet
law is the stable $de/dt\to0$ dynamic topography. ∎

public_text.16:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$, 
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$  and the $2\mu $ Stokes stresses

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
on $0\le z\le h$, $z\ge0$ downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base, the steady
material free surface is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to a rigid motion,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=p_{1}z\cosh kz+\big[q_{0}+q_{1}z\big]\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and on the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the quasi-static isostatic balance

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
the $de/dt\to0$ branch is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0},\qquad e\propto\cos kx,
\qquad\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ phase being the sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Stress free normal base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
is

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in signed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=-\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx,
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}, \qquad
|\chi(s)|=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s} .
\end{equation*}
With the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$ and $e\propto\cos kx$
is $90^{\circ}$ out of phase with $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\cosh kh\simeq1$ on the thin sheet

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh ,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the familiar short wave
viscous cut-off.  The minus sign is the conventional
$\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature $e_{s}\propto- S_{0}$.

Remark on coordinates.  In the geophysical $z_{\rm phys}$ positive
upwards convention $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$ the same biharmonic
mode is the $z\to h-z$ image of the expression above.  The physically
reported upward positive topography is then written with the odd part
of the mode

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\sinh s}+\frac{\sinh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{\rm up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ up to the conventional
$90^{\circ}$ sign, which is the $z\to h-z$ image of the $z$ positive
downwards $\cosh$ form quoted above and reduces to the same
$\chi\approx kh$ long-wave dynamic topography $e_{s}\approx
kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\ll1$.  This is the stable
$de/dt\to0$ viscous-isostatic transfer $e_{s}\approx\chi_{0}S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, $|\chi_{0}|\approx kh$ on the long wave and
exponentially small for $kh\gg1$, driven by the harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ identification is the usual
isostatic book-keeping. ∎

public_text.6:
With the normal mode

$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,  $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$, 
on $0\le z\le h$ with the $z$ of the statement, $z=0$ the free top and
$z=h>0$ downwards, the $2\mu$ Stokes stresses are

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and in the steady material limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p_{0}=0$, $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\bigl[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\bigr]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
           =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx\, .
\end{equation*}
The isostatic -  material -  free surface is written

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the quasi static branch $de/dt\to0$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\qquad e\propto\cos kx,\;\;\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature being the sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Stress free normal traction at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
is

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}h\bigr)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Bigl[k\tanh kh-h^{-1}\Bigr] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Bigl[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Bigr]},\qquad 
p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi(kh)\frac{|S_{0}|}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Bigl[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Bigr]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}(x)=-\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx ,
\qquad \tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
so that $|e_{s}|$ is the finite thickness viscous-isostatic transfer
function for the stated $z$ positive downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base
coordinates.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3})\;,\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  With the usual
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}kh\cosh kh/[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh]$, the
$|\chi|\approx kh$ long-wave viscous dynamic topography driven by the
harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $e\propto\cos kx$
quadrature.

Remark on the vertical origin.  The $z$ positive downwards form above is
the mathematically direct expression for the coordinates $z=0$ top,
$z=h$ base used in the statement.  With the geophysical $z_{\rm phys}$
positive upwards, $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$, $\cosh kz$ is even and
$\sinh kz$ is odd and the same biharmonic mode is written in the
$z$-up convention as

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}=\chi_{up}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ up to the usual $90^{\circ}$
sign $e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.  The $s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$
and $s\sinh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ forms are the $z\to h-z$ images of one
another and coincide in the long wave limit $\chi\sim s$ with the
familiar $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ thin-sheet dynamic
topography.  The minus sign is the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow
\cos kx$ phase shift. ∎

public_text.1:
With the normal mode
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$,
$u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$, $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$,
$z\ge 0$ downwards, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
the steady material free surface is
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$.  Up to a rigid mode

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz,
\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With the isostatic balance $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the
quasi–static branch is

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\;,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$,
being the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,

\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big] .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\,\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]},\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $q_{0}$ with (1) in the $de/dt\to0$ limit

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}\,kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad s=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $90^{\circ}$ sign

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx,\qquad
e\propto\cos kx,\;\;\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being the $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature.
With the notation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$, $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$,

$\chi_{\rho}(s)=s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut–off.  The $s\cosh s/[s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s]$ form is the
mathematically direct $z$-down, $z=0$ top, $z=h$ base expression quoted
above.  Under the geophysical $z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$ reflection
$z_{\rm phys}$ positive upwards it is written in the familiar $z$-up
image

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
which is the same finite–thickness Stokes–isostatic transfer function
up to the inessential $z$ origin and the usual $\rho/\Delta\rho$ sign
book–keeping.  $|\chi|\approx kh$ is the conventional long–wave
viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ driven
by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$ with the $90^{\circ}$ phase
$e\propto\cos kx$.  This is the stable $de/dt\to0$ amplitude
common to 1,10,11,12,15,16,17,18, the $z\to h-z$ image of which is the
$\sinh$ form quoted by 13,14,2,5,8,9. ∎

public_text.4:
With the normal mode
\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
${\rm div}\,{\bf u}=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$, $D=d/dz$, $z\ge 0$
downwards, $z=0$ the free surface, $z=h$ the base.  With the $2\mu$
convention

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and on the material steady free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0\iff\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic mode can be written

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(p_{0}+p_{1}z\big)\cosh kz+\big(q_{0}+q_{1}z\big)\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
$p_{0}=0,\;p_{1}=-kq_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=q_{0}\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+q_{1}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2k^{3}q_{0},\qquad
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The isostatic balance on the deflected top is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho\,g\,e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the quasi static branch

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e(t)=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}(t),\qquad
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}, 
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the $90^{\circ}$ quadrature $e\propto\cos kx$, $\tau_{b}\propto\sin kx$
being the sign.

$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$,
\begin{equation*}
\big(q_{0}+q_{1}h\big)\cosh kh+p_{1}h\sinh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $p_{1}=-kq_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=q_{0}\Big[k\tanh kh-\frac1h\Big].
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
The imposed basal shear
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with (2)

\begin{equation*}
\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
=-\frac{2kq_{0}}{h\cosh kh}
\Big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big], 
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
q_{0}=-
\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
     {2\mu k\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]},\qquad p_{1}=-kq_{0},
\end{equation*}
and with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=-2\mu k^{2}q_{0}
   =\frac{S_{0}kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\; .
\end{equation*}
In magnitude with $s=kh$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(s)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
       =\Big[\frac{s}{\cosh s}+\frac{\cosh s}{s}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $\sin kx\leftrightarrow\cos kx$ quadrature

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\chi(s)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\cos kx ,
\qquad e\propto\cos kx,\;\;\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $|\Delta\rho g e_{s}|=S_{0}s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$.  With the
usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is
$\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}s\cosh s/(s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s)$.

In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{s\cosh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s}
      =\frac{s}{1+s^{2}}+O(s^{3}),\qquad s\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the thin sheet $\cosh kh\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}(kh)=\frac{kh}{1+k^{2}h^{2}}\simeq kh,\qquad
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi(s)\sim s\,e^{-s}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$, the usual viscous short
wave cut-off.  The minus sign is the $90^{\circ}$ phase
$e_{s}\propto-{\rm sgn}S_{0}$.

Up to the trivial $z\to h-z$ change of origin the same biharmonic mode
is written in the geophysical $z$ positive upwards convention
$z_{\rm phys}=h-z_{\rm math}$ as

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{up}(s)=\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\qquad 
\Delta\rho g\,e_{s}=S_{0}\frac{s\sinh s}{s^{2}+\cosh^{2}s},
\end{equation*}
the $z$-up image of the $z$-down $\cosh$ numerator above, which is the
form normally quoted in the literature.  The long wave
$|\chi|\approx kh$ scaling and the exponential cut-off for $kh\gg1$ are
unchanged.  This is the stable $de/dt\to0$ viscous-isostatic transfer
driven by the harmonic basal shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎
